{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012ke391zrGCCkkBrZuQrBcE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017cnPSsPaQrRPyoy5uaMVYC","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 for a blind academic paper review. Your focus is **Methods + Experiments** only. Do NOT evaluate novelty or related work.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Adaptive Provenance Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\"\n\nThe paper proposes adaptive b*, a per-batch estimator for the reweighting exponent in detect-and-reweight methods for contamination-robust LM pretraining. Instead of grid-searching b on a validation set (as in Drayson et al. 2025), it derives b* by maximizing an effective tail index α_eff(b) subject to an ESS (effective sample size) constraint ESS(b) ≥ n_min.\n\nKey claims:\n1. Theoretical framework: α_eff(b) with ESS constraints is a principled method for selecting b\n2. Practical result: Adaptive b* matches or exceeds grid-search best across conditions\n3. Problem setting: Works with unknown contamination proportion using only detector scores\n\n## Method Details (Section 3)\n- Weighted loss: L = (1/Σw_j) Σ w_i ℓ(x_i; θ), w_i = (1-q_i)^b\n- α_eff(b) estimated from Pareto tail behavior of weight distribution {w_i = (1-q_i)^b}\n- b* = argmax_{b≥0} α_eff(b) s.t. ESS(b) ≥ n_min\n- ESS(b) = (Σw_i)² / Σw_i²\n- n_min = 2 throughout\n- Loss reweighting (direct gradient weighting) rather than SIR resampling\n- Algorithm 1 is a 4-step procedure per batch\n\n## Experimental Setup (Section 4)\n- Data: FineWeb subsets, 200M tokens, contamination ρ ∈ {0.4, 0.6}\n- Synthetic generated by Qwen3-0.6B (same-gen) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-gen)\n- Domain-skewed variant: 70% code, 20% tech, 10% general\n- Detectors: TF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) and ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986)\n- Models: Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B, effective batch size 16\n- 6,103 steps, 200M tokens\n- Baselines: Uniform, Grid-best (over {0.1,0.2,0.3,0.5,1.0,2.0,5.0} × 3 seeds), Detect-and-remove (q>0.5), Random reweight\n- Eval: holdout PPL (4.08M tokens), MMLU (4-shot), HellaSwag (0-shot), Self-BLEU\n\n## Results Tables\n\nTable 1: Extended grid search at ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B\n- b=0 (Uniform): 17.693±0.006\n- b=0.1: 17.680±0.005\n- b=0.2: 17.665±0.008\n- b=0.3: 17.649±0.007\n- b=0.5: 17.646±0.007\n- Paired t-test b=0.3 vs b=0.5: p=0.42 (n.s.)\n\nTable 2: Adaptive vs grid-best at ρ=0.4\n- Adaptive PPL: 17.650/17.643/17.660 (seeds 42/123/456)\n- Grid-best (b=0.5): 17.651/17.638/17.650\n- Ratios: 102.6%, 90.4%, 80.0%. Aggregate: 88.6%\n\nTable 3: Contamination scaling (seed42)\n- ρ=0.4: adaptive 17.650, Δ=0.040, 0.23% relative\n- ρ=0.6: adaptive 17.730, Δ=0.190, 1.06% relative, 4.75× amplification\n- b* median: 0.87→1.42, b* mean: 1.40→2.08\n- Ratio vs grid b=0.5: 102.6% (ρ=0.4), 111.8% (ρ=0.6)\n\nTable 4: Cross-model (seed42)\n- Qwen3-0.6B: adaptive 17.650, Δ=0.040, 0.23%\n- Llama-3.2-3B: adaptive 12.690, Δ=0.160, 1.24%, 5.39× amplification\n- b* nearly identical: median 0.87 vs 0.88\n\nTable 5: b* distribution summary\n- Various settings show b* shifts with contamination rate but is stable across model scales\n\nTable 6: n_min sensitivity (seed42)\n- n_min=1: PPL 17.668, b* median 1.73 (over-aggressive)\n- n_min=2: PPL 17.650, b* median 0.87 (optimal)\n- n_min=4 (=batch): PPL 17.690 = uniform\n- n_min=5 (>batch): PPL 17.690 = uniform\n\nTable 7: Detector quality ablation\n- TF-IDF: adaptive PPL 17.650, Δ=0.040, ratio 88.6%\n- ModernBERT: adaptive PPL 17.638, Δ=0.052, ratio 96.3%\n- b* adapts: median 0.87 (TF-IDF) vs 0.45 (ModernBERT)\n\nTable 8: Cross-generator (seed42)\n- Same-gen: Δ=0.040, b* median 0.87\n- Cross-gen (Llama→Qwen): Δ=0.050, b* median 0.83\n\nTable 9: Domain-skewed injection\n- Adaptive Δ=0.050, b* varies: code batches 1.35, general batches 0.42\n\nTable 10: Baseline comparison (seed42)\n- Uniform: 17.690\n- Random reweight: 17.695\n- Detect-and-remove: 17.668 (53.7% data)\n- Grid-best: 17.651\n- Adaptive: 17.650\n\nTable 11: Downstream (Llama-3.2-3B)\n- MMLU: 31.8→32.5 (+0.7)\n- HellaSwag: 52.1→53.2 (+1.1)\n- Self-BLEU: 0.42→0.38\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiments list includes:\n- Extended grid search at b<0.5 (3 seeds) ✓ covered in Table 1\n- ρ=0.6 data generation + uniform + adaptive + grid b=0.5 ✓ covered in Table 3\n- Llama-3.2-3B setup + uniform + adaptive ✓ covered in Table 4\n- n_min ablation (1,4,5) ✓ covered in Table 6\n- ModernBERT detector + training ✓ covered in Table 7\n- Cross-generator data + training ✓ covered in Table 8\n- Detect-and-remove baseline ✓ covered in Table 10\n- Random reweight baseline ✓ covered in Table 10\n- Domain-skewed injection ✓ covered in Table 9\n- Downstream eval (MMLU/HellaSwag) ✓ covered in Table 11\n\n## Dream Review Context\nThis is a simulated (dream) paper. The data is imagined, so focus on experimental **design** quality, not numerical precision. Do not evaluate reproducibility checklists or code availability. Do evaluate: whether experimental design supports claims, whether statistics are appropriate, whether method description is complete.\n\nMark each weakness as **idea-level** or **presentation-level**.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate:\n1. **Method description**: Is it complete? Are claims supported by experiments? Are key hyperparameters and training procedures clear?\n2. **Experimental design**: Does it cover key baselines, ablations, cross-dataset/scale dimensions?\n3. **Data presentation**: Can a reader reconstruct conclusions from tables/figures? Are error bars / statistical significance tests adequate?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary of the paper's method and experimental contributions (2-3 sentences)\n- List of Strengths (labeled S1, S2, ...)\n- List of Weaknesses (labeled W1, W2, ...) with severity (Major/Minor) and level (idea/presentation)\n- Questions for authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n- Preliminary score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Borderline Reject, 4=Borderline Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe thorough and critical. A top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3694,"cache_read_input_tokens":65276,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3694},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c76ed7d3-d15e-460a-9de5-a9e268e9219d","uuid":"deac4868-a88f-4c50-8055-cd5ba0bc2e84"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a75b9940a37278e26","tool_use_id":"toolu_017cnPSsPaQrRPyoy5uaMVYC","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 for a blind academic paper review. Your focus is **Methods + Experiments** only. Do NOT evaluate novelty or related work.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Adaptive Provenance Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\"\n\nThe paper proposes adaptive b*, a per-batch estimator for the reweighting exponent in detect-and-reweight methods for contamination-robust LM pretraining. Instead of grid-searching b on a validation set (as in Drayson et al. 2025), it derives b* by maximizing an effective tail index α_eff(b) subject to an ESS (effective sample size) constraint ESS(b) ≥ n_min.\n\nKey claims:\n1. Theoretical framework: α_eff(b) with ESS constraints is a principled method for selecting b\n2. Practical result: Adaptive b* matches or exceeds grid-search best across conditions\n3. Problem setting: Works with unknown contamination proportion using only detector scores\n\n## Method Details (Section 3)\n- Weighted loss: L = (1/Σw_j) Σ w_i ℓ(x_i; θ), w_i = (1-q_i)^b\n- α_eff(b) estimated from Pareto tail behavior of weight distribution {w_i = (1-q_i)^b}\n- b* = argmax_{b≥0} α_eff(b) s.t. ESS(b) ≥ n_min\n- ESS(b) = (Σw_i)² / Σw_i²\n- n_min = 2 throughout\n- Loss reweighting (direct gradient weighting) rather than SIR resampling\n- Algorithm 1 is a 4-step procedure per batch\n\n## Experimental Setup (Section 4)\n- Data: FineWeb subsets, 200M tokens, contamination ρ ∈ {0.4, 0.6}\n- Synthetic generated by Qwen3-0.6B (same-gen) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-gen)\n- Domain-skewed variant: 70% code, 20% tech, 10% general\n- Detectors: TF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) and ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986)\n- Models: Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B, effective batch size 16\n- 6,103 steps, 200M tokens\n- Baselines: Uniform, Grid-best (over {0.1,0.2,0.3,0.5,1.0,2.0,5.0} × 3 seeds), Detect-and-remove (q>0.5), Random reweight\n- Eval: holdout PPL (4.08M tokens), MMLU (4-shot), HellaSwag (0-shot), Self-BLEU\n\n## Results Tables\n\nTable 1: Extended grid search at ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B\n- b=0 (Uniform): 17.693±0.006\n- b=0.1: 17.680±0.005\n- b=0.2: 17.665±0.008\n- b=0.3: 17.649±0.007\n- b=0.5: 17.646±0.007\n- Paired t-test b=0.3 vs b=0.5: p=0.42 (n.s.)\n\nTable 2: Adaptive vs grid-best at ρ=0.4\n- Adaptive PPL: 17.650/17.643/17.660 (seeds 42/123/456)\n- Grid-best (b=0.5): 17.651/17.638/17.650\n- Ratios: 102.6%, 90.4%, 80.0%. Aggregate: 88.6%\n\nTable 3: Contamination scaling (seed42)\n- ρ=0.4: adaptive 17.650, Δ=0.040, 0.23% relative\n- ρ=0.6: adaptive 17.730, Δ=0.190, 1.06% relative, 4.75× amplification\n- b* median: 0.87→1.42, b* mean: 1.40→2.08\n- Ratio vs grid b=0.5: 102.6% (ρ=0.4), 111.8% (ρ=0.6)\n\nTable 4: Cross-model (seed42)\n- Qwen3-0.6B: adaptive 17.650, Δ=0.040, 0.23%\n- Llama-3.2-3B: adaptive 12.690, Δ=0.160, 1.24%, 5.39× amplification\n- b* nearly identical: median 0.87 vs 0.88\n\nTable 5: b* distribution summary\n- Various settings show b* shifts with contamination rate but is stable across model scales\n\nTable 6: n_min sensitivity (seed42)\n- n_min=1: PPL 17.668, b* median 1.73 (over-aggressive)\n- n_min=2: PPL 17.650, b* median 0.87 (optimal)\n- n_min=4 (=batch): PPL 17.690 = uniform\n- n_min=5 (>batch): PPL 17.690 = uniform\n\nTable 7: Detector quality ablation\n- TF-IDF: adaptive PPL 17.650, Δ=0.040, ratio 88.6%\n- ModernBERT: adaptive PPL 17.638, Δ=0.052, ratio 96.3%\n- b* adapts: median 0.87 (TF-IDF) vs 0.45 (ModernBERT)\n\nTable 8: Cross-generator (seed42)\n- Same-gen: Δ=0.040, b* median 0.87\n- Cross-gen (Llama→Qwen): Δ=0.050, b* median 0.83\n\nTable 9: Domain-skewed injection\n- Adaptive Δ=0.050, b* varies: code batches 1.35, general batches 0.42\n\nTable 10: Baseline comparison (seed42)\n- Uniform: 17.690\n- Random reweight: 17.695\n- Detect-and-remove: 17.668 (53.7% data)\n- Grid-best: 17.651\n- Adaptive: 17.650\n\nTable 11: Downstream (Llama-3.2-3B)\n- MMLU: 31.8→32.5 (+0.7)\n- HellaSwag: 52.1→53.2 (+1.1)\n- Self-BLEU: 0.42→0.38\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiments list includes:\n- Extended grid search at b<0.5 (3 seeds) ✓ covered in Table 1\n- ρ=0.6 data generation + uniform + adaptive + grid b=0.5 ✓ covered in Table 3\n- Llama-3.2-3B setup + uniform + adaptive ✓ covered in Table 4\n- n_min ablation (1,4,5) ✓ covered in Table 6\n- ModernBERT detector + training ✓ covered in Table 7\n- Cross-generator data + training ✓ covered in Table 8\n- Detect-and-remove baseline ✓ covered in Table 10\n- Random reweight baseline ✓ covered in Table 10\n- Domain-skewed injection ✓ covered in Table 9\n- Downstream eval (MMLU/HellaSwag) ✓ covered in Table 11\n\n## Dream Review Context\nThis is a simulated (dream) paper. The data is imagined, so focus on experimental **design** quality, not numerical precision. Do not evaluate reproducibility checklists or code availability. Do evaluate: whether experimental design supports claims, whether statistics are appropriate, whether method description is complete.\n\nMark each weakness as **idea-level** or **presentation-level**.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate:\n1. **Method description**: Is it complete? Are claims supported by experiments? Are key hyperparameters and training procedures clear?\n2. **Experimental design**: Does it cover key baselines, ablations, cross-dataset/scale dimensions?\n3. **Data presentation**: Can a reader reconstruct conclusions from tables/figures? Are error bars / statistical significance tests adequate?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary of the paper's method and experimental contributions (2-3 sentences)\n- List of Strengths (labeled S1, S2, ...)\n- List of Weaknesses (labeled W1, W2, ...) with severity (Major/Minor) and level (idea/presentation)\n- Questions for authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n- Preliminary score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Borderline Reject, 4=Borderline Acce