{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JaY4o4JPc7XvoyKRZ6A53F","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Adaptive b* 不仅免调参匹配 grid-best，在多数设置下达到或超过 grid-best。 b* 轨迹随污染率/模型规模自适应偏移，证明\n2\t  α_eff(b) 框架捕捉了正确的统计量。 Claim 2 可升级为 \"adaptive matches or exceeds grid-best\"， Claim\n3\t  1 的理论框架有 b*-tracks-optimum 的直接证据支撑。 效应放大数据直接回应 \"small effect size\" 的 reviewer concern。\n4\t  论文可正面 claim: principled, zero-cost, and effective。\n5\t\n6\t  '\n7\timagined_experiments:\n8\t- name: Grid-b low (b=0.1/0.2/0.3), seed42 (ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B)\n9\t  plan_id: p0_grid_b_low_seed42\n10\t  description: 在 b=0.5 以下扩展 grid 搜索，确认 grid-best 不在搜索边界外。seed42。\n11\t  expected_results:\n12\t    b0_1_eval_ppl: 17.681\n13\t    b0_2_eval_ppl: 17.668\n14\t    b0_3_eval_ppl: 17.654\n15\t    b0_1_train_loss: 8.42\n16\t    b0_2_train_loss: 8.95\n17\t    b0_3_train_loss: 9.28\n18\t    total_steps: 6103\n19\t    gpu_hours: 10.8\n20\t  result_rationale: 'b=0.1 极其温和的 downweighting 仅比 uniform (17.690) 好 0.009 PPL——大部分合成样本权重仍接近\n21\t    1.0，几乎无过滤效果。 b=0.2 改善至 17.668，因为 w=(1-q)^0.2 对高 q 值样本（synthetic, q≈0.85）的权重降至\n22\t    (0.15)^0.2≈0.72，开始起效但幅度不足。 b=0.3 达到 17.654，与 b=0.5 的 17.651 仅差 0.003，因为在 TF-IDF\n23\t    detector 的 q-score 分布下（bimodal, 中间区域占 46%）， b=0.3-0.5 区间的有效样本量 ESS 和 α_eff 都在最优平台内。\n24\t    train_loss 逐步上升因为更高 b 对 contaminated samples 的 loss contribution 压缩更多。\n25\t\n26\t    '\n27\t  tables:\n28\t  - 'Table 2: Extended grid-b PPL curve (b=0.1...5.0) × 3 seeds — seed42 列'\n29\t  figures_description:\n30\t  - 'Figure 2 (b-PPL curve): b=0.1/0.2/0.3 扩展 seed42 曲线左侧，确认 broad minimum 在 b∈[0.3,0.5]'\n31\t  analysis: 'seed42 的 b=0.3 与 b=0.5 差距仅 0.003 PPL，远小于 seed variance (~0.01)。 证实 b=0.5\n32\t    不是边界伪影，最优区间是宽阔的 [0.3, 0.5] 平台。\n33\t\n34\t    '\n35\t- name: Grid-b low (b=0.1/0.2/0.3), seed123 (ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B)\n36\t  plan_id: p0_grid_b_low_seed123\n37\t  description: Finer grid 扩展 seed123，验证跨 seed 一致性。\n38\t  expected_results:\n39\t    b0_1_eval_ppl: 17.674\n40\t    b0_2_eval_ppl: 17.656\n41\t    b0_3_eval_ppl: 17.641\n42\t    b0_1_train_loss: 8.43\n43\t    b0_2_train_loss: 8.96\n44\t    b0_3_train_loss: 9.29\n45\t    total_steps: 6103\n46\t    gpu_hours: 10.8\n47\t  result_rationale: 'seed123 在 MVP 中一贯表现最优（grid-best b=0.5 PPL=17.638 最低），因其随机初始化恰好对\n48\t    data quality 信号更敏感。 b=0.3 的 17.641 与 b=0.5 的 17.638 仅差 0.003，同样在 seed variance\n49\t    内。 与 seed42 一致地确认最优平台而非单一最优点。\n50\t\n51\t    '\n52\t  tables:\n53\t  - 'Table 2: Extended grid-b PPL curve — seed123 列'\n54\t  figures_description:\n55\t  - 'Figure 2: seed123 曲线叠加，三条曲线在 b∈[0.3,0.5] 重合验证平台稳定性'\n56\t  analysis: '跨 seed 一致性：seed123 的 b=0.3 同样在平台内，最优区间 cross-seed 稳定。\n57\t\n58\t    '\n59\t- name: Grid-b low (b=0.1/0.2/0.3), seed456 (ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B)\n60\t  plan_id: p0_grid_b_low_seed456\n61\t  description: Finer grid 扩展 seed456，完成 3-seed b<0.5 曲线。\n62\t  expected_results:\n63\t    b0_1_eval_ppl: 17.684\n64\t    b0_2_eval_ppl: 17.67\n65\t    b0_3_eval_ppl: 17.653\n66\t    b0_1_train_loss: 8.4\n67\t    b0_2_train_loss: 8.93\n68\t    b0_3_train_loss: 9.26\n69\t    total_steps: 6103\n70\t    gpu_hours: 10.8\n71\t  result_rationale: 'seed456 在 MVP 中 uniform PPL 略高 (17.700)，对应更大的改善空间。 b=0.3 的 17.653\n72\t    与 b=0.5 的 17.650 仅差 0.003，第三次独立确认 [0.3, 0.5] 平台。 所有三个 seed 的 b=0.3 vs b=0.5 差异一致地小于\n73\t    seed variance (σ≈0.008)。\n74\t\n75\t    '\n76\t  tables:\n77\t  - 'Table 2: Extended grid-b PPL curve — seed456 列'\n78\t  figures_description:\n79\t  - 'Figure 2: seed456 曲线完成三 seed 覆盖'\n80\t  analysis: '3/3 seeds 确认 b=0.3 和 b=0.5 在噪声范围内无显著差异，消除 boundary concern。\n81\t\n82\t    '\n83\t- name: Post-hoc eval for all 9 b<0.5 checkpoints\n84\t  plan_id: p0_grid_b_low_eval\n85\t  description: 对 9 个 b<0.5 训练 checkpoint 统一进行 holdout 评估，获取可比的 eval_ppl。\n86\t  expected_results:\n87\t    all_9_eval_ppl_collected: true\n88\t    cross_seed_mean_b0_1: 17.68\n89\t    cross_seed_mean_b0_2: 17.665\n90\t    cross_seed_mean_b0_3: 17.649\n91\t    cross_seed_mean_b0_5: 17.646\n92\t    cross_seed_std_b0_3: 0.007\n93\t    cross_seed_std_b0_5: 0.007\n94\t    b0_3_vs_b0_5_paired_t_pvalue: 0.42\n95\t    updated_finer_grid_best_b: 0.5\n96\t    finer_grid_best_b_note: b=0.3 within seed variance; b=0.5 retained as grid-best\n97\t    eval_holdout_tokens: 4077068\n98\t  result_rationale: '统一 post-hoc eval 消除训练时评估条件差异。跨 seed 平均后 b=0.3 (17.649) 与 b=0.5\n99\t    (17.646) 差异仅 0.003， paired t-test p=0.42 远不显著。这确认 grid-best 仍为 b=0.5（或更准确地说，b∈[0.3,0.5]\n100\t    是一个无显著差异的最优平台）。 关键发现是曲线形态：b=0→0.3 单调下降，b=0.3→0.5 平台，b=0.5→1.0 急剧上升。 这种不对称性（慢进快出）解释了为什么\n101\t    adaptive b* 的 median=0.87 仍然有效——b 偏高 0.4 的 PPL 代价远小于偏低 0.4 的代价。\n102\t\n103\t    '\n104\t  tables:\n105\t  - 'Table 2: 完整 extended grid-b PPL curve (b=0.1...5.0) × 3 seeds — 汇总行'\n106\t  - 'Table 2 注脚: paired t-test b=0.3 vs b=0.5, p=0.42 (n.s.)'\n107\t  figures_description:\n108\t  - 'Figure 2: 完整 b-PPL 曲线（9 个新数据点 + 18 个 MVP 数据点），标注 [0.3,0.5] 平台区域和 adaptive b*\n109\t    分布直方图叠加'\n110\t  - 'Figure 2 inset: 放大 b∈[0,1] 区域的曲线形态，标注 asymmetric penalty structure'\n111\t  analysis: 'b=0.5 boundary concern 已完全消除。最优区间是 [0.3, 0.5] 宽平台。 adaptive b* 的 p25=0.54\n112\t    恰好落在平台右沿，56% 的 per-batch b* 在 [0.3, 1.5] 区间。 结合不对称 penalty structure（偏高 < 偏低的代价），adaptive\n113\t    的 median=0.87 是合理的保守选择。 论文可表述为 \"grid-best is not a single point but a plateau\n114\t    spanning b∈[0.3, 0.5]; adaptive b* allocates the majority of its mass in or near\n115\t    this region.\" 更新 aggregate ratio（使用 b=0.5 作为 grid-best，维持 88.6%——finer grid 不改变此数字因为\n116\t    b=0.5 仍为 grid-best）。\n117\t\n118\t    '\n119\t- name: Data generation for ρ=0.6 (Qwen3-0.6B, 200M tokens)\n120\t  plan_id: p0_contamination_60_datagen\n121\t  description: 生成 60% 污染率的训练数据集，并用 TF-IDF+LR detector 进行评分。\n122\t  expected_results:\n123\t    scored_data_ready: true\n124\t    contamination_ratio_actual: 0.6\n125\t    total_tokens: 200000000\n126\t    q_score_mean: 0.68\n127\t    q_score_std: 0.35\n128\t    q_score_median: 0.78\n129\t    mid_range_ratio: 0.41\n130\t    detector_auc_on_60pct: 0.936\n131\t    datagen_gpu_hours: 3.8\n132\t  result_rationale: '60% 污染率意味着更多高 q-score 样本。q_score_mean 从 40% 的 0.60 升至 0.68，median\n133\t    从 0.707 升至 0.78， 因为 synthetic 样本占比增大推高了分布的上半部分。 detector AUC 略降至 0.936（vs 40%\n134\t    的 0.939），因为 60% 意味着 class imbalance 从 40:60 变为 60:40， TF-IDF 在 prevalence shift\n135\t    下 AUC 微降但仍 >0.93。 mid_range_ratio (q∈[0.2,0.8]) 从 0.463 降至 0.41，因为更多样本被推向高 q 区域。\n136\t\n137\t    '\n138\t  tables: []\n139\t  figures_description:\n140\t  - 'Appendix: q-score 分布对比图 (ρ=0.4 vs ρ=0.6)，标注 median 和 mean shift'\n141\t  analysis: '数据质量验证：60% 的 q-score 分布如预期偏移，detector 在不同 prevalence 下保持 AUC >0.93。 mid_range\n142\t    比例下降意味着更多样本的 contamination status 是明确的（高 q 或低 q）， 有利于 adaptive b* 的 α_eff 估计精度。\n143\t\n144\t    '\n145\t- name: Uniform baseline (ρ=0.6, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42)\n146\t  plan_id: p0_contamination_60_uniform\n147\t  description: 60% 污染率下的 uniform 基线，作为效应放大的参考点。\n148\t  expected_results:\n149\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 17.92\n150\t    eval_loss: 2.886\n151\t    train_loss: 7.48\n152\t    total_steps: 6103\n153\t    train_runtime_s: 12000\n154\t    eval_holdout_tokens: 4077068\n155\t  result_rationale: '从 40% (17.690) 到 60% uniform PPL 上升至 17.92 (+0.23 PPL, +1.3%)。\n156\t    更多合成数据意味着 60% 的梯度来自被 generate model 统计特性扭曲的文本， 累积的分布偏移导致 model 对 clean holdout\n157\t    distribution 的拟合退化。 上升幅度非线性：40% 时 PPL 比 clean (~17.45 估计) 高 0.24，60% 时高 0.47。\n158\t    这符合 strong model collapse 文献的预测——污染效应超线性增长。 train_loss 略高于 40% 的 7.31 因为合成数据的\n159\t    loss landscape 更平坦。\n160\t\n161\t    '\n162\t  tables:\n163\t  - 'Table 3: Contamination scaling (ρ=0.4 vs ρ=0.6) — Uniform 行'\n164\t  figures_description:\n165\t  - 'Figure 3: Contamination rate vs PPL bar chart — uniform 系列'\n166\t  analysis: '60% uniform PPL 比 40% 差 0.23 PPL，确认 contamination 对模型质量的负面影响随比例非线性增大。\n167\t    这为 effect amplification argument 建立了必要的 baseline gap。\n168\t\n169\t    '\n170\t- name: Adaptive b* (ρ=0.6, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42)\n171\t  plan_id: p0_contamination_60_adaptive\n172\t  description: 60% 污染下的 adaptive b*，验证效应放大 + b* 轨迹追踪。\n173\t  expected_results:\n174\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 17.73\n175\t    eval_loss: 2.875\n176\t    train_loss: 7.92\n177\t    total_steps: 6103\n178\t    train_runtime_s: 11500\n179\t    ppl_improvement_over_uniform: 0.19\n180\t    relative_ppl_improvement_pct: 1.06\n181\t    effect_amplification_vs_40pct: 4.75\n182\t    b_star_mean: 2.08\n183\t    b_star_median: 1.42\n184\t    b_star_std: 2.45\n185\t    b_star_p25: 0.82\n186\t    b_star_p75: 2.55\n187\t    b_star_pct_below_0.5: 12.3\n188\t    b_star_pct_0.5_to_1: 24.8\n189\t    b_star_pct_1_to_2: 32.1\n190\t    b_star_pct_above_2: 30.8\n191\t    ratio_vs_grid_b0_5: 111.8\n192\t    eval_holdout_tokens: 4077068\n193\t  result_rationale: '核心结果：PPL 从 17.92 (uniform) 降至 17.73，改善 0.19 PPL (1.06% relative)。\n194\t    对比 40% 的 0.04 PPL (0.23%)，效应放大 4.75x——在 [3, 5] 的 best 档范围内。\n195\t\n196\t    效应放大的因果链： 1) 60% 污染 → 每个 batch 中 ~60% 样本需要 downweight（vs 40%） 2) 更多 contaminated\n197\t    samples → α_eff(b) 的 Pareto tail 更重 → b* 自然上调以维持 ESS 3) 更高 b* → 对 synthetic samples\n198\t    的 gradient contribution 压缩更彻底 4) 有效训练信号中 clean data 占比提高 → holdout PPL 改善更大\n199\t\n200\t    b* 分布右移：median 从 0.87 (40%) 升至 1.42 (60%)，mean 从 1.40 升至 2.08。 这是 b*-tracks-optimum\n201\t    的直接证据——当 contamination 增加时，adaptive 框架自动推高 b* 以匹配新的最优区域。 p25 从 0.54 升至 0.82 意味着即使最低四分位也不再落在\n202\t    40% 的最优平台 [0.3, 0.5] 内， 而是适应性地转向 60% 的更高最优区间。\n203\t\n204\t    ratio_vs_grid_b0_5 = 111.8%：adaptive 的 0.19 PPL 改善超过 grid b=0.5 在 60% 下的预期改善 (0.10)。\n205\t    这是因为 b=0.5 在 60% 下已不再最优——contamination 增加要求更高的 b， 而 adaptive 自动追踪到了更优的 b 区间，grid\n206\t    b=0.5 做不到。\n207\t\n208\t    '\n209\t  tables:\n210\t  - 'Table 3: Contamination scaling — Adaptive 行'\n211\t  - 'Table 4: b* distribution summary across settings (40% vs 60%)'\n212\t  figures_description:\n213\t  - 'Figure 3: Effect amplification — adaptive 改善量 bar，标注 4.75x amplification'\n214\t  - 'Figure 4: b* distribution histograms side-by-side (ρ=0.4 vs ρ=0.6)，标注 median\n215\t    shift 0.87→1.42'\n216\t  - 'Figure 5: b* trajectory over training steps at ρ=0.6，与 ρ=0.4 对比'\n217\t  analysis: '这是 best 档的核心证据之一。两个关键 takeaway： (1) Effect amplification 4.75x 直接回应 \"small\n218\t    effect size\" 的 reviewer concern—— 0.3% → 1.06% 的 relative improvement 在 60% 条件下已不再\n219\t    \"small\"，且趋势表明更高污染下价值更大。 (2) b* median shift 从 0.87 到 1.42 是 α_eff(b) 框架 captured\n220\t    the right statistics 的硬证据。 b* 不是固定的超参数——它是数据驱动的自适应量，且其自适应方向与 grid-optimal shift\n221\t    一致。 这支撑 Claim 1（理论框架有效性）和 Claim 2 的升级（\"matches or exceeds\"）。\n222\t\n223\t    '\n224\t- name: Grid-b=0.5 (ρ=0.6, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42)\n225\t  plan_id: p0_contamination_60_grid_b0_5\n226\t  description: 用 40% 最优的 b=0.5 在 60% 下运行，展示 fixed-b 在 contamination shift 下的退化。\n227\t  expected_results:\n228\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 17.83\n229\t    eval_loss: 2.881\n230\t    train_loss: 9.72\n231\t    total_steps: 6103\n232\t    train_runtime_s: 12500\n233\t    ppl_improvement_over_uniform: 0.09\n234\t    relative_ppl_improvement_pct: 0.5\n235\t    ratio_of_60_vs_40_improvement: 2.25\n236\t    eval_holdout_tokens: 4077068\n237\t  result_rationale: 'b=0.5 在 60% 污染下仍优于 uniform (17.83 vs 17.92, 改善 0.09 PPL)，但改善量仅为\n238\t    adaptive 在同条件下 (0.19 PPL) 的 47%。\n239\t\n240\t    原因：b=0.5 在 60% 下 \"太温和\"。对 synthetic samples (q≈0.85)，w=(1-0.85)^0.5≈0.39， 这在 40%\n241\t    污染时足够（只需轻度过滤 40% 的数据），但在 60% 污染时不够—— 60% 的梯度仍有 39% 的权重，过滤力度不足。 grid-optimal 在\n242\t    60% 约为 b=1.5（对应 w=(0.15)^1.5≈0.058，几乎完全过滤 synthetic gradient）。\n243\t\n244\t    这个实验的价值在于直接展示 fixed-b 跨条件迁移的失败： 40% 的 grid-best 不是 60% 的 grid-best。 如果使用 Drayson\n245\t    的 validation-based b 选择，需要在 60% 条件下重新做 grid search。 Adaptive b* 自动处理了这个迁移。\n246\t\n247\t    '\n248\t  tables:\n249\t  - 'Table 3: Contamination scaling — Grid b=0.5 行'\n250\t  figures_description:\n251\t  - 'Figure 3: bar chart 中 grid b=0.5 在 60% 下的 bar 显著低于 adaptive'\n252\t  analysis: '这是 adaptive vs fixed-b 对比的关键证据。b=0.5 在 40% 是最优的，在 60% 沦为次优。 adaptive\n253\t    b* 无需 re-tuning 就适应了新条件。 论文表述：\"\"A fixed b tuned for one contamination level does\n254\t    not transfer; adaptive b* adapts automatically.\"\"\n255\t\n256\t    '\n257\t- name: Llama-3.2-3B environment setup + data prep\n258\t  plan_id: p0_llama3b_setup\n259\t  description: 下载 Llama-3.2-3B，生成 detector scores，准备训练数据。\n260\t  expected_results:\n261\t    model_downloaded: true\n262\t    model_params_B: 3.21\n263\t    vram_usage_estimate_GB: 72\n264\t    gradient_checkpointing_needed: true\n265\t    effective_batch_size: 16\n266\t    micro_batch_size: 4\n267\t    gradient_accumulation_steps: 4\n268\t    detector_scores_reused: true\n269\t    detector_note: Same TF-IDF+LR detector scores (text-based, model-agnostic)\n270\t    setup_gpu_hours: 0.5\n271\t  result_rationale: 'Llama-3.2-3B 约 3.2B 参数，FP16 下占 ~6.4GB 模型权重，但训练时 optimizer states\n272\t    + gradients + activations 需要 ~65-72GB。4× RTX 6000 Pro 96GB 可用 DDP，单卡 batch_size=4\n273\t    + gradient_accumulation=4 达到 effective batch_size=16（与 0.6B 一致，保证可比性）。\n274\t\n275\t    TF-IDF+LR detector scores 可复用——detector 基于文本特征（词频 + 逻辑回归）， 与训练模型架构无关。这也符合 Claim\n276\t    3 (unknown proportion) 的设定。\n277\t\n278\t    '\n279\t  tables: []\n280\t  figures_description: []\n281\t  analysis: '环境就绪。关键设计决策：保持 effective batch_size=16 以确保 b* 分布可直接与 0.6B 对比。 gradient\n282\t    checkpointing 增加约 20% 训练时间但不影响收敛。\n283\t\n284\t    '\n285\t- name: Uniform baseline (ρ=0.4, Llama-3.2-3B, seed42, 200M tokens)\n286\t  plan_id: p0_llama3b_uniform\n287\t  description: 3B 模型的 uniform 基线。预期比 0.6B 低得多的 PPL（更强模型）。\n288\t  expected_results:\n289\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 12.85\n290\t    eval_loss: 2.554\n291\t    train_loss: 5.89\n292\t    total_steps: 6103\n293\t    train_runtime_s: 38000\n294\t    gpu_hours: 11.5\n295\t    eval_holdout_tokens: 4077068\n296\t  result_rationale: '3B 模型在 200M tokens 上训练后 holdout PPL 约 12.85——显著低于 0.6B 的 17.69。\n297\t    PPL 降低来自更大的模型容量：更多参数可以更好地建模语言分布。 但 40% synthetic contamination 仍在拖累：clean 3B PPL\n298\t    估计约 12.50， contamination 导致 0.35 PPL degradation (~2.7%)——比 0.6B 的 1.4% degradation\n299\t    更大， 因为 3B 模型有更多参数来\"记忆\"合成数据的 distributional artifacts。\n300\t\n301\t    train_runtime 约 38000s (10.5h)：3B 的每步计算量约为 0.6B 的 5.3x， 但 gradient checkpointing\n302\t    的 recompute 增加约 20% overhead。\n303\t\n304\t    '\n305\t  tables:\n306\t  - 'Table 5: Cross-model results (Qwen3-0.6B vs Llama-3.2-3B) — Uniform 行'\n307\t  figures_description:\n308\t  - 'Figure 6: Cross-model comparison bar chart — uniform baselines'\n309\t  analysis: '3B uniform PPL 12.85 确立了 cross-model 基线。 contamination degradation 比例\n310\t    (2.7%) 大于 0.6B (1.4%)，预示 reweighting 的效益更大。\n311\t\n312\t    '\n313\t- name: Adaptive b* (ρ=0.4, Llama-3.2-3B, seed42, 200M tokens)\n314\t  plan_id: p0_llama3b_adaptive\n315\t  description: 3B 模型上的 adaptive b*——验证跨模型规模泛化 + 效应放大。\n316\t  expected_results:\n317\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 12.69\n318\t    eval_loss: 2.541\n319\t    train_loss: 6.12\n320\t    total_steps: 6103\n321\t    train_runtime_s: 39500\n322\t    gpu_hours: 12.0\n323\t    ppl_improvement_over_uniform: 0.16\n324\t    relative_ppl_improvement_pct: 1.24\n325\t    effect_amplification_vs_06B: 5.39\n326\t    b_star_mean: 1.41\n327\t    b_star_median: 0.88\n328\t    b_star_std: 1.97\n329\t    b_star_p25: 0.54\n330\t    b_star_p75: 1.53\n331\t    b_star_pct_below_0.5: 20.8\n332\t    b_star_pct_0.5_to_1: 35.6\n333\t    b_star_pct_1_to_2: 27.4\n334\t    b_star_pct_above_2: 16.2\n335\t    eval_holdout_tokens: 4077068\n336\t  result_rationale: 'PPL 改善 0.16 (12.85→12.69, 1.24% relative)——是 0.6B 的 0.04 PPL\n337\t    (0.23%) 的 5.4x 放大。\n338\t\n339\t    效应放大的机制： (1) 3B 的更大容量使其对 data quality 更敏感——更多参数 = 更精细地适配训练分布， 因此 clean vs contaminated\n340\t    distribution 的差异被放大 (2) 3B 在 contaminated samples 上学到更强的 spurious patterns（更高的\n341\t    memorization capacity）， reweighting 的去除效果更显著 (3) clean data 的信号在 3B 上被更充分地利用（0.6B\n342\t    可能 capacity-bound，无法完全受益于更干净的梯度）\n343\t\n344\t    b* 分布几乎与 0.6B 完全一致（median 0.88 vs 0.87, mean 1.41 vs 1.40）—— 这是因为 b* 仅从 per-batch\n345\t    q-score 统计量推导，与模型架构/规模无关。 同一数据 + 同一 detector + 同一 batch composition = 同一 b*。 但同样的\n346\t    b* 在 3B 上产生更大的 PPL 改善——b* 的 universal applicability 是最强的 selling point。\n347\t\n348\t    '\n349\t  tables:\n350\t  - 'Table 5: Cross-model results — Adaptive 行'\n351\t  - 'Table 4: b* distribution summary — Llama-3.2-3B 列（与 Qwen3-0.6B 并列）'\n352\t  figures_description:\n353\t  - 'Figure 6: Cross-model bar chart — adaptive bars 标注 5.4x amplification'\n354\t  - 'Figure 4: b* distribution overlay (0.6B vs 3B)——几乎完全重合，标注 KL divergence ≈ 0'\n355\t  - 'Figure 7: Scatter plot — model size vs relative PPL improvement, 标注 scaling trend'\n356\t  analysis: '三个层面的强证据： (1) 效应放大 5.4x 彻底回应 \"small effect size\"——1.24% relative PPL\n357\t    improvement on 3B is substantial (2) b* 分布跨模型规模完全一致——证明方法是 model-agnostic，b* 是数据属性而非模型属性\n358\t    (3) 无需 per-model validation——Drayson 在 1.7B 用 b=1 vs ≤360M 用 b=10，需要 per-model\n359\t    tuning； 我们的 adaptive b* 跨 0.6B 和 3B 自动给出一致且有效的值\n360\t\n361\t    Claim 2 在 3B 上的 ratio 计算： 若 grid-best for 3B 也约 b=0.5 (同一数据)，grid-best PPL ≈ 12.71，\n362\t    则 ratio = (12.85-12.69)/(12.85-12.71) = 0.16/0.14 = 114.3%。 Adaptive 超过 grid-best——因为\n363\t    per-batch b* 在高 contamination batches 用更高 b， 在 clean batches 用低 b，优于任何 fixed b。\n364\t\n365\t    '\n366\t- name: n_min=1 ablation (ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42)\n367\t  plan_id: p0_nmin_1\n368\t  description: 极松的 ESS 约束 (n_min=1)——允许 b* 过高，可能 over-downweight。\n369\t  expected_results:\n370\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 17.668\n371\t    eval_loss: 2.873\n372\t    train_loss: 7.85\n373\t    total_steps: 6103\n374\t    train_runtime_s: 10400\n375\t    b_star_mean: 2.95\n376\t    b_star_median: 1.73\n377\t    b_star_std: 3.82\n378\t    b_star_p25: 0.71\n379\t    b_star_p75: 3.45\n380\t    b_star_pct_above_5: 12.8\n381\t    eval_holdout_tokens: 4077068\n382\t  result_rationale: 'n_min=1 允许 ESS 降至 1（即一个 batch 中理论上只需 1 个有效样本）。 这释放了 b* 的上限约束——α_eff(b)\n383\t    的最大化不再受 ESS 限制，b* 可以飙到很高值。\n384\t\n385\t    结果是 b* 分布大幅右移：median 1.73 (vs n_min=2 的 0.87)，12.8% 的 batches b*>5。 在这些高 b* batches\n386\t    中，几乎所有 contaminated samples 权重趋近于零， 有效只有 1-2 个 clean samples 贡献梯度——gradient variance\n387\t    极高。\n388\t\n389\t    PPL 17.668：比 n_min=2 的 17.650 差 0.018，但仍优于 uniform 17.690 (改善 0.022)。 方法没有彻底崩溃，因为多数\n390\t    batches 的 b* 仍在合理范围 (p25=0.71)， 只有极端 batches 出问题。这说明 n_min 在保护方法稳定性上是必要但阈值不需精确。\n391\t\n392\t    '\n393\t  tables:\n394\t  - 'Table 6: n_min sensitivity ablation (n_min = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5)'\n395\t  figures_description:\n396\t  - 'Figure 8: n_min vs eval_ppl curve — n_min=1 点标注 ''over-aggressive'''\n397\t  - 'Figure 8 inset: b* distribution comparison across n_min values'\n398\t  analysis: 'n_min=1 验证了 ESS 约束的必要性：无约束下 b* 过高导致 gradient variance 增大， PPL 退化但不崩溃\n399\t    (仍优于 uniform)。 这支持 n_min=2 作为 sweet spot 的选择——既足够宽松允许有效 downweighting， 又足够紧来防止极端\n400\t    over-filtering。\n401\t\n402\t    '\n403\t- name: n_min=4 ablation (=batch_size, ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42)\n404\t  plan_id: p0_nmin_4\n405\t  description: n_min=batch_size → ESS 约束要求所有样本保持有效 → b* 被强制为 0。\n406\t  expected_results:\n407\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 17.69\n408\t    eval_loss: 2.873\n409\t    train_loss: 7.31\n410\t    total_steps: 6103\n411\t    train_runtime_s: 10300\n412\t    b_star_mean: 0.0\n413\t    b_star_median: 0.0\n414\t    b_star_std: 0.0\n415\t    b_star_pct_zero: 100.0\n416\t    equivalent_to_uniform: true\n417\t    eval_holdout_tokens: 4077068\n418\t  result_rationale: '当 n_min = batch_size = 4 时（micro-batch for DDP, per-GPU），ESS\n419\t    约束要求 每个 batch 的有效样本量 ≥ batch_size。由于 ESS 定义为 (Σw_i)²/Σw_i²， 对于 w=(1-q)^b，任何 b>0\n420\t    都会降低 contaminated samples 的权重， 从而使 ESS < batch_size。因此 b* 被强制钳制为 0。\n421\t\n422\t    PPL = 17.690，与 uniform (17.690) 完全一致——验证了当 ESS 约束完全绑定时 算法正确退化为 uniform training。train_loss\n423\t    也与 uniform 一致 (7.31)。\n424\t\n425\t    这是一个 sanity check：证明 adaptive b* 的改善确实来自非零的 b*（即 reweighting）， 而非算法实现中的其他副作用（如\n426\t    batch shuffling, gradient scaling 等）。\n427\t\n428\t    '\n429\t  tables:\n430\t  - 'Table 6: n_min sensitivity — n_min=4 行，标注 ''=uniform'''\n431\t  figures_description:\n432\t  - 'Figure 8: n_min=4 点与 uniform baseline 重合'\n433\t  analysis: '完美的退化验证。n_min=4 → b*=0 → uniform，PPL 精确匹配。 这排除了任何 confounding factor，确认改善纯粹来自\n434\t    adaptive reweighting signal。\n435\t\n436\t    '\n437\t- name: n_min=5 ablation (>batch_size, ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42)\n438\t  plan_id: p0_nmin_5\n439\t  description: n_min > batch_size 的退化情况。\n440\t  expected_results:\n441\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 17.69\n442\t    eval_loss: 2.873\n443\t    train_loss: 7.31\n444\t    total_steps: 6103\n445\t    train_runtime_s: 10300\n446\t    b_star_mean: 0.0\n447\t    b_star_median: 0.0\n448\t    b_star_std: 0.0\n449\t    b_star_pct_zero: 100.0\n450\t    equivalent_to_uniform: true\n451\t    eval_holdout_tokens: 4077068\n452\t  result_rationale: 'n_min=5 > batch_size=4，ESS 约束不可能被满足（ESS 上限为 batch_size=4 < n_min=5），\n453\t    因此 b* 始终被钳制为 0。结果与 n_min=4 完全相同——uniform training。\n454\t\n455\t    这是 boundary case 验证。n_min > batch_size 在实践中没有意义， 但作为 ablation 的一端提供完整的 sensitivity\n456\t    curve。\n457\t\n458\t    '\n459\t  tables:\n460\t  - 'Table 6: n_min sensitivity — n_min=5 行，标注 ''degenerate'''\n461\t  figures_description:\n462\t  - 'Figure 8: n_min=5 与 n_min=4 重合在 uniform baseline'\n463\t  analysis: '与 n_min=4 一致的退化行为。完整 ablation 展示清晰的 phase transition： n_min=1 (over-aggressive)\n464\t    → n_min=2 (optimal) → n_min=3 (fragile, seed-dependent) → n_min≥4 (degenerate/uniform)。\n465\t    结合 MVP 已有数据 (n_min=2: 17.650, n_min=3: 17.690 for seed42)， sensitivity curve 有\n466\t    4 个有效数据点 + 1 个 degenerate 确认。 n_min=2 是唯一稳定工作的值——但这不是 \"another hyperparameter\n467\t    to tune\"， 而是一个有明确物理含义的约束（\"每 batch 至少 2 个有效样本\"）， 且 robustness window 为 n_min∈{1,2}（两个值都\n468\t    > uniform）。\n469\t\n470\t    '\n471\t- name: ModernBERT detector training (AUC≈0.99)\n472\t  plan_id: p1_detector_modernbert\n473\t  description: 训练高 AUC detector (ModernBERT-150M) 并生成训练集的 q-scores。\n474\t  expected_results:\n475\t    detector_auc: 0.986\n476\t    detector_accuracy: 0.961\n477\t    detector_ece_calibrated: 0.022\n478\t    temperature_T: 0.85\n479\t    q_score_mean: 0.58\n480\t    q_score_std: 0.4\n481\t    q_score_median: 0.72\n482\t    mid_range_ratio: 0.28\n483\t    training_gpu_hours: 1.8\n484\t    scoring_gpu_hours: 0.5\n485\t  result_rationale: 'ModernBERT (150M params) 的 AUC 达 0.986（vs TF-IDF 的 0.939）， 因为\n486\t    transformer 可以捕捉 n-gram 之外的语义一致性和 generation artifacts。 calibrated ECE 0.022 (vs\n487\t    TF-IDF 0.048) 因为模型输出 logit 的温度校准更精确。\n488\t\n489\t    关键差异在 q-score 分布：mid_range_ratio 仅 0.28 (vs TF-IDF 0.463)。 ModernBERT 将更多样本推向极端（要么\n490\t    q≈0 (confident real) 要么 q≈1 (confident synthetic)）， 中间地带大幅缩小。这意味着 w=(1-q)^b 的权重更接近\n491\t    binary (0 or 1)。\n492\t\n493\t    '\n494\t  tables:\n495\t  - 'Table 7: Detector comparison (TF-IDF+LR vs ModernBERT)'\n496\t  figures_description:\n497\t  - 'Appendix Figure: q-score 分布对比 (TF-IDF vs ModernBERT)——ModernBERT 更 bimodal'\n498\t  analysis: 'ModernBERT 的更高 AUC 和更 bimodal 的 q-score 分布将显著改变 b* 的行为。 mid_range_ratio\n499\t    降至 0.28 意味着 adaptive b* 的空间被压缩—— 多数样本的 (1-q)^b 对 b 的敏感度降低（接近 0 或 1）。\n500\t\n501\t    '\n502\t- name: Training with ModernBERT scores (ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42)\n503\t  plan_id: p1_detector_ablation_training\n504\t  description: 用 ModernBERT q-scores 训练 adaptive + uniform，量化 detector quality → PPL\n505\t    关系。\n506\t  expected_results:\n507\t    modernbert_adaptive_eval_ppl: 17.638\n508\t    modernbert_uniform_eval_ppl: 17.69\n509\t    modernbert_adaptive_improvement: 0.052\n510\t    modernbert_adaptive_relative_pct: 0.29\n511\t    tfidf_adaptive_improvement: 0.04\n512\t    improvement_ratio_modernbert_vs_tfidf: 1.3\n513\t    modernbert_b_star_mean: 0.82\n514\t    modernbert_b_star_median: 0.45\n515\t    modernbert_b_star_std: 1.15\n516\t    modernbert_grid_best_b: 0.5\n517\t    modernbert_grid_best_ppl: 17.634\n518\t    modernbert_adaptive_ratio: 96.3\n519\t    gpu_hours: 7.0\n520\t  result_rationale: '更高 AUC detector → 更精确的 q-scores → 更准确的 reweighting → 更低的 PPL。\n521\t    ModernBERT adaptive PPL 17.638 vs TF-IDF adaptive 17.650，改善 0.012 PPL。 相对改善量从\n522\t    0.040 (TF-IDF) 增至 0.052 (ModernBERT)，提升 30%。\n523\t\n524\t    但关键 finding 是：这个 30% 提升对应 AUC 从 0.939 到 0.986 的差距 (0.047 AUC)， 边际收益递减。TF-IDF (AUC=0.939)\n525\t    已捕获了大部分 reweighting value。\n526\t\n527\t    b* 分布因 q-score 更 bimodal 而更窄更低：median 0.45 (vs TF-IDF 0.87)。 这是因为 ModernBERT 的\n528\t    q 值更极端，(1-q)^b 的权重区分在低 b 就已足够。 adaptive 框架自动适应了 detector 质量的差异——这本身就是 adaptivity\n529\t    的体现。\n530\t\n531\t    '\n532\t  tables:\n533\t  - 'Table 7: Detector quality ablation — 训练结果列'\n534\t  - 'Table 4: b* distribution — ModernBERT 列'\n535\t  figures_description:\n536\t  - 'Figure 9: Detector AUC vs PPL improvement scatter/bar chart'\n537\t  - 'Figure 4: b* distributions under different detectors (TF-IDF vs ModernBERT)'\n538\t  analysis: 'Two findings for paper: (1) Lightweight detector sufficiency：TF-IDF+LR\n539\t    (AUC=0.939) 已实现 77% 的 ModernBERT (AUC=0.986) reweighting value， 但成本仅为后者的 1/100（CPU\n540\t    训练 vs GPU 微调 150M transformer）。 (2) Adaptive b* detector-agnostic：方法在两种截然不同的 detector\n541\t    下都有效工作， b* 分布自动适应 q-score 分布特性。 这强化了 Claim 1（方法的 principled nature）和实用性论证。\n542\t\n543\t    '\n544\t- name: Cross-generator data (Llama-3.2-1B generates, Qwen3-0.6B trains)\n545\t  plan_id: p1_cross_generator_datagen\n546\t  description: 用不同模型家族生成合成数据，测试 cross-generator robustness。\n547\t  expected_results:\n548\t    generator_model: Llama-3.2-1B\n549\t    training_model: Qwen3-0.6B\n550\t    contamination_ratio: 0.4\n551\t    total_tokens: 200000000\n552\t    detector_auc_cross_gen: 0.952\n553\t    detector_accuracy_cross_gen: 0.873\n554\t    q_score_mean: 0.62\n555\t    q_score_std: 0.38\n556\t    q_score_median: 0.73\n557\t    mid_range_ratio: 0.44\n558\t    datagen_gpu_hours: 7.5\n559\t  result_rationale: 'Llama-3.2-1B 生成的 synthetic text 与 Qwen3-0.6B 生成的有不同的 stylistic\n560\t    signatures。 TF-IDF+LR detector 在 cross-generator 场景下 AUC 提高至 0.952 (vs same-generator\n561\t    0.939)—— 因为跨模型族的 generation artifacts 更容易被 n-gram 特征区分 （Llama 和 Qwen 有不同的 tokenizer、训练数据、生成模式）。\n562\t\n563\t    q-score 分布略有不同：mean 0.62 (vs 0.60)，median 0.73 (vs 0.707)。 Llama 生成的文本被检测器更 confidently\n564\t    识别为 synthetic，对应稍高的 q 值。\n565\t\n566\t    '\n567\t  tables: []\n568\t  figures_description:\n569\t  - 'Appendix: q-score distribution comparison (same-gen vs cross-gen)'\n570\t  analysis: 'Cross-generator 数据准备就绪。detector 在 cross-generator 场景下自然表现更好， 因为跨模型族的\n571\t    artifacts 差异更大。这是一个 favorable setup， 但也反映了 real-world 场景（web corpus 含多个生成器的输出）。\n572\t\n573\t    '\n574\t- name: Cross-generator training (adaptive + uniform, ρ=0.4)\n575\t  plan_id: p1_cross_generator_training\n576\t  description: 用 Llama 生成的 contaminated data 训练 Qwen3-0.6B，测试 adaptive 跨生成器鲁棒性。\n577\t  expected_results:\n578\t    cross_gen_uniform_ppl: 17.72\n579\t    cross_gen_adaptive_ppl: 17.67\n580\t    cross_gen_improvement: 0.05\n581\t    cross_gen_relative_pct: 0.28\n582\t    cross_gen_b_star_mean: 1.32\n583\t    cross_gen_b_star_median: 0.83\n584\t    cross_gen_b_star_std: 1.86\n585\t    same_gen_improvement_for_ref: 0.04\n586\t    cross_gen_vs_same_gen_ratio: 1.25\n587\t    gpu_hours: 7.0\n588\t  result_rationale: 'Cross-generator contamination 的 uniform PPL (17.72) 比 same-generator\n589\t    (17.69) 略高—— Llama-generated text 的 distributional distance 与 Qwen training 的\n590\t    mismatch 更大。\n591\t\n592\t    Adaptive 改善 0.05 PPL (0.28% relative)，与 same-generator 的 0.04 PPL (0.23%) 相当甚至略好。\n593\t    改善略大因为 cross-generator 的 contamination 更容易检测 (AUC=0.952 vs 0.939)， q-scores 更\n594\t    discriminative → reweighting 更精确。\n595\t\n596\t    b* 分布与 same-generator 高度一致 (median 0.83 vs 0.87)， 证明 adaptive 框架不依赖于特定生成器的 q-score\n597\t    特征。\n598\t\n599\t    '\n600\t  tables:\n601\t  - 'Table 8: Cross-generator results (same-gen vs cross-gen)'\n602\t  figures_description:\n603\t  - 'Figure 4: b* distribution comparison (same-gen vs cross-gen)——高度重合'\n604\t  analysis: 'Adaptive b* 在跨生成器场景下稳定工作，改善量与 same-generator 一致。 这验证了 Claim 3 的扩展：方法不仅不需要已知污染比例，\n605\t    也不需要知道 contamination 来源模型——只需 detector scores 即可。 论文可以表述为 \"generator-agnostic:\n606\t    adaptive works regardless of the source of synthetic contamination.\"\n607\t\n608\t    '\n609\t- name: Detect-and-remove baseline (threshold=0.5, ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42)\n610\t  plan_id: p1_baseline_detect_remove\n611\t  description: 硬阈值过滤 baseline——q>0.5 的样本直接丢弃。\n612\t  expected_results:\n613\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 17.668\n614\t    eval_loss: 2.8725\n615\t    train_loss: 6.85\n616\t    total_steps: 3540\n617\t    retained_data_fraction: 0.537\n618\t    retained_tokens: 107400000\n619\t    ppl_improvement_over_uniform: 0.022\n620\t    relative_ppl_improvement_pct: 0.12\n621\t    improvement_fraction_of_adaptive: 0.55\n622\t    gpu_hours: 2.1\n623\t  result_rationale: 'q>0.5 阈值移除 46.3% 的数据（mid_range + high-q 部分）。 仅保留 53.7% 的训练 token\n624\t    (~107M)，total_steps 从 6103 降至 ~3540。\n625\t\n626\t    PPL 17.668——优于 uniform (17.690, -0.022) 但逊于 adaptive (17.650, 差 +0.018)。 改善量仅为\n627\t    adaptive 的 55%。\n628\t\n629\t    原因有二： (1) 数据损失：detector 不完美 (AUC=0.939)，mid_range 中有大量 clean-but-borderline 样本被错误移除。\n630\t    移除 46% 数据意味着训练集有效缩减近半，loss of coverage 影响 language model 的 distributional coverage。\n631\t    (2) 丧失细粒度：hard threshold 是 binary 决策，而 adaptive reweighting 保留了 gradient-level\n632\t    的连续控制。 q=0.48 (刚好保留) 和 q=0.52 (刚好移除) 的样本被处理方式截然不同，但其实它们的 contamination 概率差异极小。\n633\t\n634\t    train_loss 低于其他方法因为数据更干净（移除了大部分 noisy samples）但数据量也更少。\n635\t\n636\t    '\n637\t  tables:\n638\t  - 'Table 9: Baseline comparison (uniform, grid-best, adaptive, detect-remove, random-reweight)'\n639\t  figures_description:\n640\t  - 'Figure 10: Baseline comparison bar chart — detect-and-remove 标注 data loss penalty'\n641\t  analysis: 'Detect-and-remove 的 0.12% PPL 改善仅为 adaptive 的 55%， 清晰展示 soft reweighting\n642\t    优于 hard filtering。 关键 insight：在 imperfect detector 下 (AUC<1.0)， 连续权重比二元决策更能容忍检测误差。\n643\t    This directly supports the \"loss reweighting > SIR resampling\" positioning vs\n644\t    Drayson。\n645\t\n646\t    '\n647\t- name: Random reweighting baseline (ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42)\n648\t  plan_id: p1_baseline_random_reweight\n649\t  description: 随机权重 baseline——验证 detector signal 是 load-bearing 的。\n650\t  expected_results:\n651\t    eval_ppl_holdout: 17.695\n652\t    eval_loss: 2.874\n653\t    train_loss: 7.28\n654\t    total_steps: 6103\n655\t    train_runtime_s: 10400\n656\t    ppl_vs_uniform: -0.005\n657\t    ppl_vs_adaptive: -0.045\n658\t    random_weight_mean: 0.5\n659\t    random_weight_std: 0.29\n660\t    gpu_hours: 3.4\n661\t  result_rationale: '随机权重 (i.i.d. uniform [0,1]) 不含任何 contamination signal。 PPL 17.695\n662\t    与 uniform 17.690 几乎无差异 (差 0.005, <0.03% relative)—— 随机噪声在 expectation 上等价于 uniform\n663\t    weighting，仅增加少量 gradient variance。\n664\t\n665\t    微弱的退化 (0.005 PPL 劣于 uniform) 来自随机权重引入的 gradient variance： 高权重 batch 和低权重 batch\n666\t    的 loss 贡献不均衡，增加了 optimization 的随机性。 但效应极小，证明 reweighting 本身不有害（只要权重均值合理）。\n667\t\n668\t    与 adaptive 的 0.045 PPL 差距证明：adaptive 的改善 100% 来自 detector-informed weighting，\n669\t    不是来自 \"任何形式的 reweighting 都有帮助\" 的偏见。\n670\t\n671\t    '\n672\t  tables:\n673\t  - 'Table 9: Baseline comparison — Random reweight 行'\n674\t  figures_description:\n675\t  - 'Figure 10: Random reweight bar 与 uniform 重合，凸显 adaptive 的 detector-driven gap'\n676\t  analysis: '最清晰的 ablation 之一：random reweight ≈ uniform → detector signal 是必要条件。 adaptive\n677\t    的改善不是来自 \"diversity of weights\" 或 \"regularization effect\"， 而是精准地识别和 downweight\n678\t    contaminated samples。 论文必须 include 此 baseline 以消除 trivial explanation。\n679\t\n680\t    '\n681\t- name: Domain-skewed injection (code-heavy contamination, ρ=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B)\n682\t  plan_id: p2_domain_skewed\n683\t  description: 合成数据集中注入 code/technical 域，测试 adaptive 在非均匀域分布下的鲁棒性。\n684\t  expected_results:\n685\t    contamination_distribution: 70% code, 20% technical/science, 10% general\n686\t    uniform_eval_ppl: 17.73\n687\t    adaptive_eval_ppl: 17.68\n688\t    adaptive_improvement: 0.05\n689\t    relative_ppl_improvement_pct: 0.28\n690\t    b_star_mean: 1.52\n691\t    b_star_median: 0.95\n692\t    b_star_std: 2.12\n693\t    b_star_per_domain_code_median: 1.35\n694\t    b_star_per_domain_general_median: 0.42\n695\t    gpu_hours: 6.5\n696\t  result_rationale: 'Domain-skewed contamination（70% code）使 uniform PPL 比 uniform\n697\t    random injection (17.69) 略高 (17.73)， 因为 code-heavy contamination 在 code domain\n698\t    上引入更严重的 distributional shift， 同时在 general domain 影响较小——overall holdout PPL 反映了这种\n699\t    domain-imbalanced 退化。\n700\t\n701\t    Adaptive 改善 0.05 PPL (0.28% relative)——与 uniform injection (0.23%) 相当。 关键是 b*\n702\t    的 per-batch 自适应能力： code-heavy batch（contamination 集中）的 b* median 更高 (1.35)， general-domain\n703\t    batch（contamination 稀疏）的 b* 更低 (0.42)。 这体现了 per-batch b* 的核心优势——不是对所有数据用同一个 b，\n704\t    而是根据每个 batch 的 contamination 程度动态调整。\n705\t\n706\t    Fixed b 方法在这种 skewed 场景下尤其受限： 对 code domain 最优的 b 对 general domain 过度 downweight，反之亦然。\n707\t\n708\t    '\n709\t  tables:\n710\t  - 'Table 10: Domain-skewed injection results'\n711\t  figures_description:\n712\t  - 'Figure 11: Per-domain b* distribution (code vs general)——展示 domain-adaptive behavior'\n713\t  - 'Appendix: Domain-specific PPL breakdown'\n714\t  analysis: 'Domain-skewed 实验强化了 per-batch b* 的 story： 方法不仅追踪 contamination rate 的变化（P0.2），还追踪\n715\t    contamination 的 domain 分布。 这是 \"b is not a hyperparameter — it''s a data-driven\n716\t    quantity\" 叙事的最强证据之一。 对 reviewer 来说：\"real web corpora have domain-skewed contamination,\n717\t    not uniform injection; adaptive b* handles both.\"\n718\t\n719\t    '\n720\t- name: Downstream eval (MMLU/HellaSwag on Llama-3.2-3B checkpoints)\n721\t  plan_id: p2_downstream_eval\n722\t  description: 在 3B model checkpoints 上评估下游任务，验证 PPL 改善是否转移。\n723\t  expected_results:\n724\t    uniform_mmlu_acc: 31.8\n725\t    adaptive_mmlu_acc: 32.5\n726\t    mmlu_delta_abs_pct: 0.7\n727\t    mmlu_delta_relative_pct: 2.2\n728\t    uniform_hellaswag_acc: 52.1\n729\t    adaptive_hellaswag_acc: 53.2\n730\t    hellaswag_delta_abs_pct: 1.1\n731\t    hellaswag_delta_relative_pct: 2.1\n732\t    uniform_selfbleu_4gram: 0.42\n733\t    adaptive_selfbleu_4gram: 0.38\n734\t    selfbleu_improvement: 0.04\n735\t    gpu_hours: 1.8\n736\t  result_rationale: 'PPL 改善 (12.85→12.69, 1.24% relative) 预期部分转移至下游任务。\n737\t\n738\t    MMLU (知识密集型，4-shot)： 3B 在 200M tokens 上训练的 MMLU 基线约 31.8%（远未饱和）， adaptive 提升至\n739\t    32.5% (+0.7 abs)。 机制：更干净的预训练 → factual knowledge 被更准确地编码， 而非被 synthetic data 的\n740\t    hallucinated facts 干扰。 δ=0.7% 在 [0.3, 1.5] 的 best 档范围内。\n741\t\n742\t    HellaSwag (常识推理，0-shot)： Uniform 52.1%，adaptive 53.2% (+1.1 abs)。 改善比 MMLU 更大因为\n743\t    HellaSwag 更依赖 language model quality (sentence completion)， 而 PPL 正是 language\n744\t    model quality 的直接度量。 δ=1.1% 在 [0.3, 1.5] 范围内偏高，反映 3B 模型上的 amplified effect。\n745\t\n746\t    Self-BLEU (generation diversity)： 0.42 → 0.38 (↓0.04)——更低 = 更多样。 Adaptive 减少了\n747\t    synthetic data 导致的 repetitive patterns，生成文本更多样。\n748\t\n749\t    '\n750\t  tables:\n751\t  - 'Table 11: Downstream evaluation (uniform vs adaptive, Llama-3.2-3B)'\n752\t  figures_description:\n753\t  - 'Figure 12: Downstream task improvement bar chart (MMLU, HellaSwag, Self-BLEU)'\n754\t  - 'Figure 7: Extended with downstream metrics — model size vs downstream Δ'\n755\t  analysis: 'PPL→downstream 转移验证了方法的实际价值。 MMLU +0.7%、HellaSwag +1.1% 绝对改善在 200M tokens\n756\t    的有限预训练下可观。 Self-BLEU 改善进一步证明 adaptive reweighting 减少了 model collapse 征兆（repetitive\n757\t    outputs）。\n758\t\n759\t    Reviewer concern \"does PPL matter for real tasks?\" → 是的， 且效应在下游比 PPL 上更可测量（0.7-1.1%\n760\t    absolute vs 1.24% relative PPL）， 因为下游任务评估了不同维度的模型能力。\n761\t\n762\t    论文表述：\"Adaptive b* improves not only perplexity but also downstream task performance,\n763\t    confirming that the cleaner gradient signal during pretraining transfers to measurable\n764\t    gains in knowledge recall (MMLU +0.7%) and commonsense reasoning (HellaSwag +1.1%).\"'\n765\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017cnPSsPaQrRPyoy5uaMVYC","session_id":"c76ed7d3-d15e-460a-9de5-a9e268e9219d","uuid":"09362525-f690-47da-a037-970febe3b05e","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:08:13.749Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012yTkLEb3a8bH5UMMB9ooGm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RoUFmQGoZKm9mcaQWyJBDW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_10/projects/provenance_weight_training/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T011902/best/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1707,"cache_read_input_tokens":39470,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1707,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":70,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017cnPSsPaQrRPyoy5uaMVYC","session_id":"c76ed7d3-d15e-460a-9de5-a9e268e9219d","uuid":"4b515f1a-1a37-4030-a413-1fd3097460b5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RoUFmQGoZKm9mcaQWyJBDW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n9\t\\usepackage{algorithmic}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n13\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Adaptive Provenance Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\n19\t\\date{}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t%==============================================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\t%==============================================================================\n27\tWeb corpora used for language model pretraining increasingly contain synthetic text of unknown provenance. Detect-and-reweight methods assign sample weights $w_i=(1-q_i)^b$ based on detector scores $q_i$, but the exponent $b$ must be tuned per model via grid search on a clean validation set. We propose \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: a per-batch estimator that derives the optimal exponent from an effective tail-index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of the within-batch score distribution, subject to an effective-sample-size (ESS) constraint. The method requires no validation set, no grid search, and no knowledge of the contamination proportion. Across two model scales (Qwen3-0.6B, Llama-3.2-3B), two contamination rates (40\\%, 60\\%), cross-generator injection, domain-skewed contamination, and two detector qualities, adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds the grid-search optimum (aggregate ratio $\\geq$88.6\\% at $\\rho$=0.4; surpasses grid-best at $\\rho$=0.6 and on 3B). The $b^*$ trajectory shifts automatically with contamination rate and model scale, providing direct evidence that the $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework captures the correct data-driven statistics. Effect amplification from 0.23\\% relative PPL improvement (0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4) to 1.24\\% (3B, $\\rho$=0.4) addresses the small-effect-size concern. Downstream gains on MMLU (+0.7\\%) and HellaSwag (+1.1\\%) confirm transfer beyond perplexity.\n28\t\\end{abstract}\n29\t\n30\t%==============================================================================\n31\t\\section{Introduction}\n32\t\\label{sec:intro}\n33\t%==============================================================================\n34\t\n35\tLarge language models are pretrained on web-scraped corpora that increasingly contain machine-generated text. Estimates suggest 20--60\\% of web text may be synthetically generated, and the Strong Model Collapse result~\\citep{shumailov2024strong} demonstrates that even small amounts of synthetic contamination can degrade scaling laws. This motivates principled methods for contamination-robust pretraining.\n36\t\n37\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} introduced the detect-and-reweight paradigm: given per-sample contamination scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$ from a binary detector, assign weights $w_i = (1-q_i)^b$ and resample the training data via Sampling Importance Resampling (SIR). Their approach is effective but requires the exponent $b$ to be selected empirically per model (e.g., $b=10$ for GPT-2 $\\leq$360M, $b=1$ for SmolLM-1.7B) using a clean validation set---a resource that is unavailable in large-scale web pretraining.\n38\t\n39\tWe make two observations. First, the convergence theory of~\\citet{convergence2025} provides a bound $d(\\hat{\\mathbb{P}}_t, \\mathbb{P}_0) \\lesssim t^{-\\min\\{p,\\alpha\\}}$ that connects the tail index $\\alpha$ of the data distribution to the rate at which reweighted training converges to the clean distribution. This motivates defining an \\emph{effective tail index} $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ as a function of the weight exponent. Second, the effective sample size (ESS) of a weighted batch provides a natural constraint against over-aggressive downweighting.\n40\t\n41\tWe propose \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: for each training batch, we estimate $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from the empirical distribution of $q$-scores, then select $b^*$ to maximize $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ subject to ESS $\\geq n_{\\min}$. This replaces grid search with a closed-form per-batch computation, eliminates the need for a validation set, and adapts automatically to varying contamination levels within and across datasets.\n42\t\n43\tOur contributions are:\n44\t\\begin{enumerate}\n45\t    \\item \\textbf{Theoretical framework.} We introduce $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ estimation with ESS constraints as a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent, replacing per-model empirical tuning.\n46\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical result.} Adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds grid-search best across model scales, contamination rates, generators, and detector qualities, at zero tuning cost.\n47\t    \\item \\textbf{Problem setting.} Unlike Golden Ratio Weighting (which requires known contamination proportion $k$) and unlike validation-based $b$ selection, our method operates with unknown contamination proportion using only detector scores.\n48\t\\end{enumerate}\n49\t\n50\t%==============================================================================\n51\t\\section{Related Work}\n52\t\\label{sec:related}\n53\t%==============================================================================\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{Model Collapse and Synthetic Contamination.}\n56\t\\citet{shumailov2024strong} prove that recursive training on model-generated data causes ``strong model collapse,'' where the tails of the data distribution are progressively lost. This motivates contamination mitigation even when synthetic fractions are small.\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Detect-and-Reweight.}\n59\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} train a binary detector (ModernBERT, AUC=0.986) and weight samples as $w_i=(1-q_i)^b$ via SIR resampling. The exponent $b$ is selected per model on a validation set ($b=10$ for $\\leq$360M models, $b=1$ for 1.7B). Our work adopts the same weighting formula but replaces the empirical $b$ with a principled adaptive $b^*$ and implements direct loss reweighting rather than SIR.\n60\t\n61\t\\paragraph{Data Mixing and Reweighting.}\n62\tDoReMi~\\citep{xie2023doremi} and DSIR~\\citep{xie2023dsir} optimize domain-level mixing weights; SoftDedup~\\citep{abbas2024softdedup} reweights by duplication degree. These operate at coarser granularity than our instance-level approach. Dynamic loss-based reweighting~\\citep{mindermann2024prioritized} uses per-sample loss rather than detector scores, offering a complementary signal.\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{Golden Ratio Weighting.}\n65\t\\citet{goldenratio2025} derive a theoretically optimal weight $w^*=1/\\varphi$ under the assumption that the real/synthetic mixing proportion $k$ is known. Our setting assumes $k$ is unknown.\n66\t\n67\t%==============================================================================\n68\t\\section{Method}\n69\t\\label{sec:method}\n70\t%==============================================================================\n71\t\n72\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n73\t\n74\tLet $\\mathcal{D} = \\{x_1, \\ldots, x_N\\}$ be a pretraining corpus containing a mixture of human-written and machine-generated text at an unknown contamination proportion $\\rho$. A pretrained binary detector assigns scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$ where higher values indicate higher probability of being synthetic. We train a language model by minimizing the weighted loss:\n75\t\\begin{equation}\n76\t    \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{\\sum_j w_j} \\sum_{i=1}^{|\\mathcal{B}|} w_i \\, \\ell(x_i; \\theta), \\quad w_i = (1 - q_i)^b,\n77\t\\end{equation}\n78\twhere $\\mathcal{B}$ is a training batch and $\\ell$ is the per-token cross-entropy loss. The exponent $b \\geq 0$ controls the downweighting aggressiveness: $b=0$ recovers uniform training, while $b \\to \\infty$ approaches hard filtering.\n79\t\n80\t\\subsection{Effective Tail Index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$}\n81\t\n82\tMotivated by the convergence bound $d(\\hat{\\mathbb{P}}_t, \\mathbb{P}_0) \\lesssim t^{-\\min\\{p,\\alpha\\}}$ of~\\citet{convergence2025}, we define an effective tail index that captures how well a given $b$ suppresses contaminated gradient contributions. For a batch $\\mathcal{B}$ with empirical $q$-score distribution, we estimate $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from the Pareto tail behavior of the weight distribution $\\{w_i = (1-q_i)^b\\}_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}}$. Intuitively, higher $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ means faster convergence to the clean distribution.\n83\t\n84\t\\subsection{Adaptive $b^*$ with ESS Constraint}\n85\t\n86\tWe select $b^*$ per batch by solving:\n87\t\\begin{equation}\n88\t    b^* = \\arg\\max_{b \\geq 0} \\; \\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b) \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad \\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq n_{\\min},\n89\t\\end{equation}\n90\twhere the effective sample size is:\n91\t\\begin{equation}\n92\t    \\mathrm{ESS}(b) = \\frac{\\left(\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} w_i\\right)^2}{\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} w_i^2}.\n93\t\\end{equation}\n94\t\n95\tThe ESS constraint prevents pathological over-downweighting: without it, the optimizer would choose arbitrarily large $b$, reducing the batch to a single effective sample and causing gradient variance explosion.\n96\t\n97\tWe set $n_{\\min} = 2$ throughout. This choice has a clear interpretation (``at least 2 effective samples per batch'') and is robust: Section~\\ref{sec:nmin_ablation} shows that $n_{\\min} \\in \\{1, 2\\}$ both improve over uniform, $n_{\\min} = 3$ is seed-sensitive, and $n_{\\min} \\geq 4$ (i.e., $\\geq$ batch size) degenerates to uniform training.\n98\t\n99\t\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n100\t\\caption{Adaptive $b^*$ Reweighting}\n101\t\\label{alg:adaptive}\n102\t\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n103\t\\REQUIRE Batch $\\mathcal{B} = \\{(x_i, q_i)\\}$, ESS lower bound $n_{\\min}$\n104\t\\STATE Compute $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from $\\{q_i\\}_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}}$ via Pareto tail estimation\n105\t\\STATE Find $b^* = \\arg\\max_{b \\geq 0} \\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ subject to $\\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq n_{\\min}$\n106\t\\STATE Compute weights $w_i = (1 - q_i)^{b^*}$\n107\t\\STATE Return weighted loss $\\mathcal{L} = \\frac{\\sum_i w_i \\, \\ell(x_i;\\theta)}{\\sum_i w_i}$\n108\t\\end{algorithmic}\n109\t\\end{algorithm}\n110\t\n111\t\\subsection{Loss Reweighting vs.\\ SIR Resampling}\n112\t\n113\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} implement reweighting via SIR: samples are drawn with probability proportional to $w_i$, then trained with uniform loss. We instead apply weights directly to the gradient:\n114\t$\\nabla_\\theta \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{\\sum_j w_j} \\sum_i w_i \\nabla_\\theta \\ell(x_i; \\theta)$.\n115\tThis preserves continuous, fine-grained control at the gradient level, avoids the discretization inherent in resampling, and enables per-batch $b^*$ without data preprocessing.\n116\t\n117\t%==============================================================================\n118\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n119\t\\label{sec:setup}\n120\t%==============================================================================\n121\t\n122\t\\paragraph{Data.}\n123\tWe construct contaminated pretraining corpora from FineWeb subsets (200M tokens). At contamination rate $\\rho \\in \\{0.4, 0.6\\}$, we replace $\\rho$ fraction of the data with synthetic text generated by Qwen3-0.6B (same-generator) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-generator). A domain-skewed variant injects 70\\% code, 20\\% technical/science, 10\\% general among the synthetic portion.\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Detectors.}\n126\tOur primary detector is TF-IDF + Logistic Regression (AUC=0.939, calibrated ECE=0.048). We also evaluate ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986, ECE=0.022) as a high-quality alternative.\n127\t\n128\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n129\tWe train Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B from pretrained checkpoints. Effective batch size is 16 for both (micro-batch 4 $\\times$ gradient accumulation 4), with gradient checkpointing for 3B. Training runs 6,103 steps on 200M tokens.\n130\t\n131\t\\paragraph{Baselines.}\n132\t\\textbf{Uniform}: standard training ($b=0$). \\textbf{Grid-best}: best $b$ from grid search over $\\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\\}$ with 3 seeds. \\textbf{Detect-and-remove}: hard filtering at $q > 0.5$. \\textbf{Random reweight}: i.i.d.\\ uniform $[0,1]$ weights (no detector signal).\n133\t\n134\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n135\tPrimary: holdout perplexity (PPL) on 4.08M tokens. Secondary: MMLU (4-shot), HellaSwag (0-shot), Self-BLEU (4-gram). All experiments use 3 random seeds (42, 123, 456) where applicable.\n136\t\n137\t%==============================================================================\n138\t\\section{Results}\n139\t\\label{sec:results}\n140\t%==============================================================================\n141\t\n142\t\\subsection{Grid Search Landscape and the Optimal Plateau}\n143\t\\label{sec:grid}\n144\t\n145\tTable~\\ref{tab:grid} presents the extended grid search over $b \\in \\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\\}$ at $\\rho=0.4$ on Qwen3-0.6B across 3 seeds. The optimal region is not a single point but a broad plateau spanning $b \\in [0.3, 0.5]$: the cross-seed mean at $b=0.3$ (17.649) and $b=0.5$ (17.646) differ by only 0.003 PPL, with a paired $t$-test $p=0.42$ (n.s., $n=3$). This plateau has an asymmetric shape---PPL decreases slowly for $b < 0.3$ (from 17.680 at $b=0.1$) but rises sharply for $b > 0.5$---implying that overshooting $b$ is less costly than undershooting.\n146\t\n147\t\\begin{table}[t]\n148\t\\centering\n149\t\\caption{Extended grid search: eval PPL ($\\downarrow$) at $\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, 200M tokens.}\n150\t\\label{tab:grid}\n151\t\\small\n152\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc c}\n153\t\\toprule\n154\t$b$ & seed42 & seed123 & seed456 & Mean $\\pm$ Std \\\\\n155\t\\midrule\n156\t0 (Uniform) & 17.690 & 17.690 & 17.700 & 17.693 $\\pm$ 0.006 \\\\\n157\t0.1 & 17.681 & 17.674 & 17.684 & 17.680 $\\pm$ 0.005 \\\\\n158\t0.2 & 17.668 & 17.656 & 17.670 & 17.665 $\\pm$ 0.008 \\\\\n159\t0.3 & 17.654 & 17.641 & 17.653 & 17.649 $\\pm$ 0.007 \\\\\n160\t\\textbf{0.5} & \\textbf{17.651} & \\textbf{17.638} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{17.646 $\\pm$ 0.007} \\\\\n161\t\\bottomrule\n162\t\\end{tabular}\n163\t\\vspace{0.3em}\n164\t\n165\t{\\footnotesize Paired $t$-test $b$=0.3 vs $b$=0.5: $p$=0.42 (n.s.). Grid-best is $b$=0.5 but the difference from $b$=0.3 is within seed variance.}\n166\t\\end{table}\n167\t\n168\t\\subsection{Adaptive $b^*$ Matches Grid-Best}\n169\t\\label{sec:adaptive_vs_grid}\n170\t\n171\tTable~\\ref{tab:adaptive_main} compares adaptive $b^*$ against the grid-best ($b=0.5$) under the same $\\rho=0.4$ setting. Adaptive achieves an aggregate ratio of 88.6\\% of the grid-best improvement (measured as PPL reduction from uniform), with all three seeds improving over uniform. Critically, adaptive requires a single training run vs.\\ $7 \\times 3 = 21$ runs for the full grid search.\n172\t\n173\t\\begin{table}[t]\n174\t\\centering\n175\t\\caption{Adaptive $b^*$ vs.\\ grid-best ($b$=0.5) at $\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B.}\n176\t\\label{tab:adaptive_main}\n177\t\\small\n178\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n179\t\\toprule\n180\tMethod & seed42 & seed123 & seed456 \\\\\n181\t\\midrule\n182\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.690 & 17.700 \\\\\n183\tGrid-best ($b$=0.5) PPL & 17.651 & 17.638 & 17.650 \\\\\n184\tAdaptive $b^*$ PPL & 17.650 & 17.643 & 17.660 \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\tRatio (\\% of grid-best gain) & 102.6\\% & 90.4\\% & 80.0\\% \\\\\n187\t\\bottomrule\n188\t\\end{tabular}\n189\t\\vspace{0.3em}\n190\t\n191\t{\\footnotesize Aggregate ratio: 88.6\\%. All 3 seeds: adaptive $>$ uniform. Adaptive uses 1 run; grid search uses $7 \\times 3 = 21$ runs.}\n192\t\\end{table}\n193\t\n194\t\\subsection{Contamination Rate Scaling: Effect Amplification}\n195\t\\label{sec:rho_scaling}\n196\t\n197\tTable~\\ref{tab:rho_scaling} compares $\\rho=0.4$ and $\\rho=0.6$ (Qwen3-0.6B, seed42). At 60\\% contamination, adaptive $b^*$ improves PPL by 0.19 (1.06\\% relative), a 4.75$\\times$ amplification over the 0.04 PPL improvement at 40\\%. Importantly, the fixed grid-best $b=0.5$ (tuned at $\\rho$=0.4) transfers poorly: it captures only 47\\% of the adaptive improvement at $\\rho$=0.6, demonstrating that a fixed $b$ does not generalize across contamination levels.\n198\t\n199\tThe $b^*$ distribution shifts rightward from median 0.87 ($\\rho$=0.4) to 1.42 ($\\rho$=0.6), confirming that the adaptive framework automatically increases downweighting strength as contamination increases. This $b^*$-tracks-optimum behavior provides direct evidence that $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ captures the correct data-driven statistics.\n200\t\n201\t\\begin{table}[t]\n202\t\\centering\n203\t\\caption{Contamination rate scaling ($\\rho$=0.4 vs $\\rho$=0.6), Qwen3-0.6B, seed42.}\n204\t\\label{tab:rho_scaling}\n205\t\\small\n206\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n207\t\\toprule\n208\t& $\\rho=0.4$ & $\\rho=0.6$ \\\\\n209\t\\midrule\n210\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.920 \\\\\n211\tGrid $b$=0.5 PPL & 17.651 & 17.830 \\\\\n212\tAdaptive $b^*$ PPL & 17.650 & 17.730 \\\\\n213\t\\midrule\n214\t$\\Delta$PPL (adaptive vs uniform) & 0.040 & 0.190 \\\\\n215\tRelative improvement (\\%) & 0.23 & 1.06 \\\\\n216\tEffect amplification & --- & 4.75$\\times$ \\\\\n217\t\\midrule\n218\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 1.42 \\\\\n219\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 2.08 \\\\\n220\tRatio vs grid $b$=0.5 (\\%) & 102.6 & 111.8 \\\\\n221\t\\bottomrule\n222\t\\end{tabular}\n223\t\\end{table}\n224\t\n225\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Scaling: Qwen3-0.6B vs Llama-3.2-3B}\n226\t\\label{sec:cross_model}\n227\t\n228\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_model} shows results on Llama-3.2-3B at $\\rho=0.4$. The 3B model exhibits 5.39$\\times$ effect amplification over 0.6B: adaptive improves PPL by 0.16 (1.24\\% relative) vs.\\ 0.04 (0.23\\%) on 0.6B. The $b^*$ distribution is nearly identical across model scales (median 0.88 vs 0.87; mean 1.41 vs 1.40), confirming that $b^*$ is a \\emph{data property}, not a model property---adaptive requires no per-model tuning.\n229\t\n230\t\\begin{table}[t]\n231\t\\centering\n232\t\\caption{Cross-model results at $\\rho$=0.4, seed42, 200M tokens.}\n233\t\\label{tab:cross_model}\n234\t\\small\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t& Qwen3-0.6B & Llama-3.2-3B \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 12.850 \\\\\n240\tAdaptive $b^*$ PPL & 17.650 & 12.690 \\\\\n241\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.040 & 0.160 \\\\\n242\tRelative improvement (\\%) & 0.23 & 1.24 \\\\\n243\tEffect amplification & --- & 5.39$\\times$ \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.88 \\\\\n246\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 1.41 \\\\\n247\t\\bottomrule\n248\t\\end{tabular}\n249\t\\end{table}\n250\t\n251\t\\subsection{$b^*$ Distribution Analysis}\n252\t\\label{sec:bstar_dist}\n253\t\n254\tTable~\\ref{tab:bstar_dist} summarizes the $b^*$ distribution across experimental conditions. Two patterns emerge: (1) $b^*$ shifts rightward with contamination rate ($\\rho$=0.6 median 1.42 vs $\\rho$=0.4 median 0.87), reflecting the need for stronger downweighting at higher contamination; (2) $b^*$ is invariant to model scale (0.6B vs 3B) and adjusts to detector quality (ModernBERT median 0.45 vs TF-IDF median 0.87, as the more precise detector requires less aggressive weighting).\n255\t\n256\t\\begin{table}[t]\n257\t\\centering\n258\t\\caption{$b^*$ distribution summary across settings.}\n259\t\\label{tab:bstar_dist}\n260\t\\small\n261\t\\begin{tabular}{l cccc}\n262\t\\toprule\n263\tSetting & Median & Mean & P25 & P75 \\\\\n264\t\\midrule\n265\tQwen-0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4, TF-IDF & 0.87 & 1.40 & 0.54 & 1.53 \\\\\n266\tQwen-0.6B, $\\rho$=0.6, TF-IDF & 1.42 & 2.08 & 0.82 & 2.55 \\\\\n267\tLlama-3.2-3B, $\\rho$=0.4, TF-IDF & 0.88 & 1.41 & 0.54 & 1.53 \\\\\n268\tQwen-0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4, ModernBERT & 0.45 & 0.82 & --- & --- \\\\\n269\tCross-gen (Llama$\\to$Qwen), $\\rho$=0.4 & 0.83 & 1.32 & --- & --- \\\\\n270\t\\bottomrule\n271\t\\end{tabular}\n272\t\\end{table}\n273\t\n274\t\\subsection{$n_{\\min}$ Sensitivity Ablation}\n275\t\\label{sec:nmin_ablation}\n276\t\n277\tTable~\\ref{tab:nmin} presents the ESS constraint sensitivity ($\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42). The results reveal a clear phase structure: $n_{\\min}=1$ is over-aggressive ($b^*$ median 1.73, 12.8\\% of batches with $b^*>5$) yet still improves over uniform (PPL 17.668); $n_{\\min}=2$ is optimal (PPL 17.650); $n_{\\min} \\geq 4$ ($\\geq$ batch size) forces $b^*=0$ and degenerates exactly to uniform (PPL 17.690). This confirms that $n_{\\min}$ is not a sensitive hyperparameter: $n_{\\min} \\in \\{1,2\\}$ both work, while values $\\geq$ batch size are degenerate by construction.\n278\t\n279\t\\begin{table}[t]\n280\t\\centering\n281\t\\caption{ESS constraint ($n_{\\min}$) sensitivity, $\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42.}\n282\t\\label{tab:nmin}\n283\t\\small\n284\t\\begin{tabular}{l cccc}\n285\t\\toprule\n286\t$n_{\\min}$ & Eval PPL & $b^*$ median & $b^*$ mean & Note \\\\\n287\t\\midrule\n288\t1 & 17.668 & 1.73 & 2.95 & Over-aggressive \\\\\n289\t\\textbf{2} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{0.87} & \\textbf{1.40} & \\textbf{Optimal} \\\\\n290\t4 (=batch) & 17.690 & 0.00 & 0.00 & $\\equiv$ Uniform \\\\\n291\t5 ($>$batch) & 17.690 & 0.00 & 0.00 & Degenerate \\\\\n292\t\\bottomrule\n293\t\\end{tabular}\n294\t\\end{table}\n295\t\n296\t\\subsection{Detector Quality Ablation}\n297\t\\label{sec:detector}\n298\t\n299\tTable~\\ref{tab:detector} compares the lightweight TF-IDF+LR detector (AUC=0.939) against ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986). ModernBERT improves adaptive PPL by an additional 0.012 (from 17.650 to 17.638), a 30\\% increase in reweighting value. However, the TF-IDF detector already captures 77\\% of the ModernBERT-level improvement at orders-of-magnitude lower cost (CPU-only vs.\\ GPU fine-tuning of a 150M transformer). This supports the practical claim that a lightweight detector suffices.\n300\t\n301\tThe $b^*$ distribution adapts to detector quality: ModernBERT's more bimodal $q$-scores (mid-range ratio 0.28 vs.\\ 0.46 for TF-IDF) lead to lower $b^*$ (median 0.45 vs.\\ 0.87), since less aggressive weighting is needed when scores are already well-separated.\n302\t\n303\t\\begin{table}[t]\n304\t\\centering\n305\t\\caption{Detector quality ablation ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n306\t\\label{tab:detector}\n307\t\\small\n308\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n309\t\\toprule\n310\t& TF-IDF+LR & ModernBERT \\\\\n311\t\\midrule\n312\tDetector AUC & 0.939 & 0.986 \\\\\n313\tCalibrated ECE & 0.048 & 0.022 \\\\\n314\tMid-range ratio ($q \\in [0.2, 0.8]$) & 0.463 & 0.280 \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.690 \\\\\n317\tAdaptive PPL & 17.650 & 17.638 \\\\\n318\t$\\Delta$PPL (improvement) & 0.040 & 0.052 \\\\\n319\tGrid-best PPL & 17.646 & 17.634 \\\\\n320\tAdaptive / Grid ratio (\\%) & 88.6 & 96.3 \\\\\n321\t\\midrule\n322\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.45 \\\\\n323\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 0.82 \\\\\n324\t\\bottomrule\n325\t\\end{tabular}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\t\\subsection{Cross-Generator Robustness}\n329\t\\label{sec:cross_gen}\n330\t\n331\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_gen} evaluates robustness when synthetic data is generated by Llama-3.2-1B (a different model family) rather than Qwen3-0.6B (the training model). The TF-IDF detector achieves higher AUC (0.952) on cross-generator data, as cross-family generation artifacts are more distinctive. Adaptive $b^*$ improves PPL by 0.05 (0.28\\% relative), comparable to the same-generator result (0.04 PPL, 0.23\\%), confirming generator-agnostic operation.\n332\t\n333\t\\begin{table}[t]\n334\t\\centering\n335\t\\caption{Cross-generator results ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n336\t\\label{tab:cross_gen}\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\t& Same-gen (Qwen) & Cross-gen (Llama) \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tDetector AUC & 0.939 & 0.952 \\\\\n343\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.720 \\\\\n344\tAdaptive PPL & 17.650 & 17.670 \\\\\n345\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.040 & 0.050 \\\\\n346\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.83 \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\end{table}\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{Domain-Skewed Contamination}\n352\t\\label{sec:domain_skew}\n353\t\n354\tReal web corpora exhibit non-uniform contamination across domains. Table~\\ref{tab:domain_skew} tests a domain-skewed injection (70\\% code, 20\\% technical, 10\\% general among synthetic data). Adaptive $b^*$ improves by 0.05 PPL (0.28\\% relative), matching uniform-injection performance. Per-batch $b^*$ varies by domain: code-heavy batches receive median $b^*=1.35$ (stronger downweighting) while general-domain batches receive 0.42, demonstrating the per-batch adaptivity that fixed-$b$ methods cannot achieve.\n355\t\n356\t\\begin{table}[t]\n357\t\\centering\n358\t\\caption{Domain-skewed injection ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B).}\n359\t\\label{tab:domain_skew}\n360\t\\small\n361\t\\begin{tabular}{l c}\n362\t\\toprule\n363\tMetric & Value \\\\\n364\t\\midrule\n365\tContamination distribution & 70\\% code / 20\\% tech / 10\\% general \\\\\n366\tUniform PPL & 17.730 \\\\\n367\tAdaptive PPL & 17.680 \\\\\n368\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.050 \\\\\n369\t$b^*$ median (code batches) & 1.35 \\\\\n370\t$b^*$ median (general batches) & 0.42 \\\\\n371\t\\bottomrule\n372\t\\end{tabular}\n373\t\\end{table}\n374\t\n375\t\\subsection{Baseline Comparison}\n376\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n377\t\n378\tTable~\\ref{tab:baselines} compares all methods at $\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42. Adaptive $b^*$ achieves the best PPL (17.650). Detect-and-remove ($q>0.5$) achieves only 55\\% of the adaptive improvement while discarding 46\\% of data. Random reweighting performs equivalently to uniform (PPL 17.695 vs 17.690), confirming that the improvement derives from detector-informed weighting, not from weight diversity or regularization effects.\n379\t\n380\t\\begin{table}[t]\n381\t\\centering\n382\t\\caption{Baseline comparison ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n383\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n384\t\\small\n385\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n386\t\\toprule\n387\tMethod & Eval PPL & $\\Delta$ vs Uniform & Data Used \\\\\n388\t\\midrule\n389\tUniform ($b$=0) & 17.690 & --- & 100\\% \\\\\n390\tRandom reweight & 17.695 & $-$0.005 & 100\\% \\\\\n391\tDetect-and-remove ($q$>0.5) & 17.668 & +0.022 & 53.7\\% \\\\\n392\tGrid-best ($b$=0.5) & 17.651 & +0.039 & 100\\% \\\\\n393\t\\textbf{Adaptive $b^*$} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{+0.040} & \\textbf{100\\%} \\\\\n394\t\\bottomrule\n395\t\\end{tabular}\n396\t\\end{table}\n397\t\n398\t\\subsection{Downstream Task Evaluation}\n399\t\\label{sec:downstream}\n400\t\n401\tTable~\\ref{tab:downstream} evaluates Llama-3.2-3B checkpoints on downstream tasks. Adaptive $b^*$ improves MMLU by +0.7\\% absolute (31.8$\\to$32.5, knowledge recall) and HellaSwag by +1.1\\% (52.1$\\to$53.2, commonsense reasoning). Self-BLEU decreases from 0.42 to 0.38, indicating reduced repetitive generation---a direct symptom of model collapse that adaptive reweighting mitigates.\n402\t\n403\t\\begin{table}[t]\n404\t\\centering\n405\t\\caption{Downstream evaluation (Llama-3.2-3B, $\\rho$=0.4, seed42).}\n406\t\\label{tab:downstream}\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\t& Uniform & Adaptive $b^*$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tMMLU (4-shot, \\%$\\uparrow$) & 31.8 & 32.5 & +0.7 \\\\\n413\tHellaSwag (0-shot, \\%$\\uparrow$) & 52.1 & 53.2 & +1.1 \\\\\n414\tSelf-BLEU (4-gram, $\\downarrow$) & 0.42 & 0.38 & $-$0.04 \\\\\n415\t\\bottomrule\n416\t\\end{tabular}\n417\t\\end{table}\n418\t\n419\t%==============================================================================\n420\t\\section{Analysis}\n421\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n422\t%==============================================================================\n423\t\n424\t\\paragraph{$b^*$ tracks the optimum.}\n425\tThe most striking finding is that the $b^*$ distribution shifts systematically with experimental conditions while requiring no manual adjustment. When contamination increases from 40\\% to 60\\%, median $b^*$ rises from 0.87 to 1.42---matching the shift in the grid-optimal region. When a more precise detector is used (ModernBERT), $b^*$ decreases to median 0.45 because less aggressive weighting suffices for better-separated scores. When contamination is domain-skewed, per-batch $b^*$ differentiates code-heavy batches (1.35) from general batches (0.42). These adaptations happen automatically through the $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework, validating that it captures the correct data-driven statistics.\n426\t\n427\t\\paragraph{Effect amplification addresses the small-effect concern.}\n428\tAt 0.6B/$\\rho$=0.4, the 0.23\\% relative PPL improvement might appear small. However, this scales substantially: 1.06\\% at $\\rho$=0.6 (4.75$\\times$) and 1.24\\% at 3B/$\\rho$=0.4 (5.39$\\times$). Downstream gains of +0.7\\% MMLU and +1.1\\% HellaSwag on 3B further demonstrate that the cleaner gradient signal during pretraining produces measurable improvements in knowledge recall and commonsense reasoning. The value proposition is principled, zero-cost, and effective: adaptive $b^*$ is strictly better than uniform at no additional tuning cost.\n429\t\n430\t\\paragraph{Lightweight detector sufficiency.}\n431\tTF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) achieves 77\\% of the reweighting value of ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986), at negligible computational cost. This is practically significant: practitioners need not train a large neural detector to benefit from provenance-aware reweighting.\n432\t\n433\t\\paragraph{Loss reweighting vs hard filtering.}\n434\tDetect-and-remove achieves only 55\\% of the adaptive improvement while discarding 46\\% of data. Under an imperfect detector (AUC $<$ 1.0), continuous weights are more tolerant of detection errors than binary decisions: a sample with $q=0.48$ (retained) and $q=0.52$ (removed) receive vastly different treatment under hard filtering but similar weights under soft reweighting.\n435\t\n436\t%==============================================================================\n437\t\\section{Conclusion}\n438\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n439\t%==============================================================================\n440\t\n441\tWe presented adaptive $b^*$, a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent in contamination-aware language model pretraining. By deriving $b^*$ from the effective tail index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of per-batch score distributions subject to an ESS constraint, our approach eliminates the need for validation-based grid search while matching or exceeding grid-optimal performance. The $b^*$ trajectory adapts automatically to contamination rate, model scale, detector quality, generator identity, and domain composition, confirming that the framework captures meaningful data statistics rather than requiring per-setting tuning.\n442\t\n443\tEffect amplification at higher contamination rates (4.75$\\times$ at $\\rho$=0.6) and larger models (5.39$\\times$ at 3B) suggests that adaptive $b^*$ becomes increasingly valuable precisely where contamination matters most. The method is principled (grounded in convergence-motivated $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ estimation), zero-cost (no validation runs), and effective (consistent improvements across all tested settings).\n444\t\n445\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n446\tOur experiments use controlled synthetic injection rather than organically contaminated web data; results on truly ``in the wild'' contamination remain to be validated. The method inherits the quality of the upstream detector---if the detector fails to distinguish real from synthetic text (e.g., for high-quality human-like generation), reweighting cannot help. We evaluate at up to 3B parameters and 200M tokens; scaling behavior at 7B+ and trillion-token scale is an open question.\n447\t\n448\t%==============================================================================\n449\t% References\n450\t%==============================================================================\n451\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n452\t\n453\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n454\t\n455\t\\bibitem[Drayson et~al.(2025)]{drayson2025contamination}\n456\tDrayson, N., et~al.\n457\t\\newblock Detect and remove: Addressing synthetic data contamination in model training.\n458\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}, 2025.\n459\t\n460\t\\bibitem[Shumailov et~al.(2024)]{shumailov2024strong}\n461\tShumailov, I., Shumaylov, Z., Zhao, Y., Gal, Y., Papernot, N., and Anderson, R.\n462\t\\newblock AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data.\n463\t\\newblock \\emph{Nature}, 2024.\n464\t\n465\t\\bibitem[{Convergence Theory}(2025)]{convergence2025}\n466\tAnonymous.\n467\t\\newblock Convergence bounds for reweighted distributions under tail-index conditions.\n468\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2602.16065}, 2025.\n469\t\n470\t\\bibitem[{Golden Ratio}(2025)]{goldenratio2025}\n471\tAnonymous.\n472\t\\newblock Golden ratio weighting for synthetic data mixtures.\n473\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2502.18049}, 2025.\n474\t\n475\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023a)]{xie2023doremi}\n476\tXie, S.~M., Pham, H., Dong, X., Du, N., Liu, H., Lu, Y., Liang, P., Le, Q.~V., Ma, T., and Yu, A.~W.\n477\t\\newblock DoReMi: Optimizing data mixtures speeds up language model pretraining.\n478\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n479\t\n480\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023b)]{xie2023dsir}\n481\tXie, S.~M., Santurkar, S., Ma, T., and Liang, P.\n482\t\\newblock Data selection for language models via importance resampling.\n483\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n484\t\n485\t\\bibitem[Abbas et~al.(2024)]{abbas2024softdedup}\n486\tAbbas, A., et~al.\n487\t\\newblock SoftDedup: Soft deduplication for pretraining data.\n488\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2024.\n489\t\n490\t\\bibitem[Mindermann et~al.(2024)]{mindermann2024prioritized}\n491\tMindermann, S., et~al.\n492\t\\newblock Prioritized training on points that are learnable, worth learning, and not yet learned.\n493\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n494\t\n495\t\\end{thebibliography}\n496\t\n497\t\\end{document}\n498\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017cnPSsPaQrRPyoy5uaMVYC","session_id":"c76ed7d3-d15e-460a-9de5-a9e268e9219d","uuid":"4a2e62e7-94ac-4db6-8a5e-7aae6a5c756a","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:08:14.280Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012ke391zrGCCkkBrZuQrBcE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PSavrw2wgVukHweXy7toJE","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 for a blind academic paper review. Your focus is **Novelty + Related Work + Story Consistency** only. Do NOT evaluate method implementation details or experimental design specifics.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Adaptive Provenance Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\"\n\nThe paper proposes adaptive b*, a per-batch estimator for the reweighting exponent in detect-and-reweight methods for contamination-robust LM pretraining. Instead of grid-searching b on a validation set (as in Drayson et al. 2025), it derives b* by maximizing an effective tail index α_eff(b) subject to an ESS constraint. Claims: (1) principled theoretical framework, (2) matches/exceeds grid-best, (3) works with unknown contamination proportion.\n\n## Paper Structure and Content\n\n### Abstract Claims:\n- Adaptive b* derives optimal exponent from α_eff(b) of within-batch score distribution with ESS constraint\n- No validation set, no grid search, no knowledge of contamination proportion needed\n- Across two model scales (0.6B, 3B), two contamination rates (40%, 60%), cross-generator, domain-skewed, two detector qualities\n- Aggregate ratio ≥88.6% at ρ=0.4; surpasses grid-best at ρ=0.6 and on 3B\n- Effect amplification from 0.23% to 1.24% addresses small-effect-size concern\n- Downstream: MMLU +0.7%, HellaSwag +1.1%\n\n### Introduction:\n- Motivates the problem via model collapse (Shumailov et al. 2024) and increasing synthetic contamination\n- Positions against Drayson et al. 2025 (detect-and-reweight with empirical b selection)\n- Two observations: (1) convergence theory provides tail-index bound, (2) ESS as natural constraint\n- Three contributions claimed: theoretical framework, practical result, problem setting (unknown ρ)\n\n### Related Work (Section 2):\n- Model Collapse: Shumailov et al. 2024\n- Detect-and-Reweight: Drayson et al. 2025 (ModernBERT, SIR, per-model b tuning)\n- Data Mixing: DoReMi (Xie et al. 2023a), DSIR (Xie et al. 2023b), SoftDedup (Abbas et al. 2024), Dynamic loss (Mindermann et al. 2024)\n- Golden Ratio Weighting: Golden Ratio 2025 (requires known k)\n\n### Method (Section 3):\n- Problem setup: weighted loss with w_i = (1-q_i)^b\n- α_eff(b) from Pareto tail estimation\n- b* = argmax α_eff(b) s.t. ESS ≥ n_min\n- Loss reweighting vs SIR resampling (argues for continuous gradient-level control)\n\n### Key Results:\n- Adaptive matches grid-best at 88.6% aggregate ratio (ρ=0.4, 0.6B)\n- Surpasses grid-best at ρ=0.6 (ratio 111.8%) and on 3B (ratio ~114%)\n- b* tracks optimum automatically across conditions\n- Effect amplification: 4.75× at ρ=0.6, 5.39× at 3B\n- Lightweight TF-IDF detector captures 77% of ModernBERT value\n- Cross-generator and domain-skewed robustness confirmed\n\n### Conclusion:\n- Reiterates principled, zero-cost, effective\n- Effect amplification at higher contamination and larger models\n- Limitations: controlled injection (not wild), inherits detector quality, only up to 3B/200M tokens\n\n### References (8 total):\n1. Drayson et al. 2025 - Detect and remove, EMNLP 2025\n2. Shumailov et al. 2024 - AI models collapse, Nature\n3. \"Convergence Theory\" [2025] - Anonymous, arXiv:2602.16065\n4. \"Golden Ratio\" [2025] - Anonymous, arXiv:2502.18049\n5. Xie et al. 2023a - DoReMi, NeurIPS\n6. Xie et al. 2023b - DSIR, NeurIPS\n7. Abbas et al. 2024 - SoftDedup, ACL\n8. Mindermann et al. 2024 - Prioritized training, ICLR\n\n## Dream Review Context\nThis is a simulated (dream) paper. Data is imagined. Do not evaluate reproducibility checklists or code availability. References may be partially fabricated (this is a pipeline limitation, not an idea problem — flag as presentation-level). Focus on: novelty positioning, related work landscape, story consistency.\n\nMark each weakness as **idea-level** or **presentation-level**.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the differentiation from existing work clear? Do contribution claims stand independently?\n2. **Related work**: Is literature coverage sufficient? Are important baselines/methods missing? Is positioning accurate?\n3. **Story consistency**: Are the claims in abstract/intro/conclusion consistent with what method/experiments actually show? Is the narrative logic self-consistent?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary of the paper's positioning and novelty (2-3 sentences)\n- List of Strengths (labeled S1, S2, ...)\n- List of Weaknesses (labeled W1, W2, ...) with severity (Major/Minor) and level (idea/presentation)\n- Questions for authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n- Preliminary score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Borderline Reject, 4=Borderline Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe thorough and critical. A top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3694,"cache_read_input_tokens":65276,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3694},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c76ed7d3-d15e-460a-9de5-a9e268e9219d","uuid":"2066fa02-46d6-4567-b441-d43c09d2e2c1"}
