{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0164kHMHBQADmyDsFUWuvsUa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n9\t\\usepackage{algorithmic}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n13\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Adaptive Provenance Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\n19\t\\date{}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t%==============================================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\t%==============================================================================\n27\tWeb corpora used for language model pretraining increasingly contain synthetic text of unknown provenance. Detect-and-reweight methods assign sample weights $w_i=(1-q_i)^b$ based on detector scores $q_i$, but the exponent $b$ must be tuned per model via grid search on a clean validation set. We propose \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: a per-batch estimator that derives the optimal exponent from an effective tail-index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of the within-batch score distribution, subject to an effective-sample-size (ESS) constraint. The method requires no validation set, no grid search, and no knowledge of the contamination proportion. Across two model scales (Qwen3-0.6B, Llama-3.2-3B), two contamination rates (40\\%, 60\\%), cross-generator injection, domain-skewed contamination, and two detector qualities, adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds the grid-search optimum (aggregate ratio $\\geq$88.6\\% at $\\rho$=0.4; surpasses grid-best at $\\rho$=0.6 and on 3B). The $b^*$ trajectory shifts automatically with contamination rate and model scale, providing direct evidence that the $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework captures the correct data-driven statistics. Effect amplification from 0.23\\% relative PPL improvement (0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4) to 1.24\\% (3B, $\\rho$=0.4) addresses the small-effect-size concern. Downstream gains on MMLU (+0.7\\%) and HellaSwag (+1.1\\%) confirm transfer beyond perplexity.\n28\t\\end{abstract}\n29\t\n30\t%==============================================================================\n31\t\\section{Introduction}\n32\t\\label{sec:intro}\n33\t%==============================================================================\n34\t\n35\tLarge language models are pretrained on web-scraped corpora that increasingly contain machine-generated text. Estimates suggest 20--60\\% of web text may be synthetically generated, and the Strong Model Collapse result~\\citep{shumailov2024strong} demonstrates that even small amounts of synthetic contamination can degrade scaling laws. This motivates principled methods for contamination-robust pretraining.\n36\t\n37\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} introduced the detect-and-reweight paradigm: given per-sample contamination scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$ from a binary detector, assign weights $w_i = (1-q_i)^b$ and resample the training data via Sampling Importance Resampling (SIR). Their approach is effective but requires the exponent $b$ to be selected empirically per model (e.g., $b=10$ for GPT-2 $\\leq$360M, $b=1$ for SmolLM-1.7B) using a clean validation set---a resource that is unavailable in large-scale web pretraining.\n38\t\n39\tWe make two observations. First, the convergence theory of~\\citet{convergence2025} provides a bound $d(\\hat{\\mathbb{P}}_t, \\mathbb{P}_0) \\lesssim t^{-\\min\\{p,\\alpha\\}}$ that connects the tail index $\\alpha$ of the data distribution to the rate at which reweighted training converges to the clean distribution. This motivates defining an \\emph{effective tail index} $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ as a function of the weight exponent. Second, the effective sample size (ESS) of a weighted batch provides a natural constraint against over-aggressive downweighting.\n40\t\n41\tWe propose \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: for each training batch, we estimate $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from the empirical distribution of $q$-scores, then select $b^*$ to maximize $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ subject to ESS $\\geq n_{\\min}$. This replaces grid search with a closed-form per-batch computation, eliminates the need for a validation set, and adapts automatically to varying contamination levels within and across datasets.\n42\t\n43\tOur contributions are:\n44\t\\begin{enumerate}\n45\t    \\item \\textbf{Theoretical framework.} We introduce $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ estimation with ESS constraints as a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent, replacing per-model empirical tuning.\n46\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical result.} Adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds grid-search best across model scales, contamination rates, generators, and detector qualities, at zero tuning cost.\n47\t    \\item \\textbf{Problem setting.} Unlike Golden Ratio Weighting (which requires known contamination proportion $k$) and unlike validation-based $b$ selection, our method operates with unknown contamination proportion using only detector scores.\n48\t\\end{enumerate}\n49\t\n50\t%==============================================================================\n51\t\\section{Related Work}\n52\t\\label{sec:related}\n53\t%==============================================================================\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{Model Collapse and Synthetic Contamination.}\n56\t\\citet{shumailov2024strong} prove that recursive training on model-generated data causes ``strong model collapse,'' where the tails of the data distribution are progressively lost. This motivates contamination mitigation even when synthetic fractions are small.\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Detect-and-Reweight.}\n59\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} train a binary detector (ModernBERT, AUC=0.986) and weight samples as $w_i=(1-q_i)^b$ via SIR resampling. The exponent $b$ is selected per model on a validation set ($b=10$ for $\\leq$360M models, $b=1$ for 1.7B). Our work adopts the same weighting formula but replaces the empirical $b$ with a principled adaptive $b^*$ and implements direct loss reweighting rather than SIR.\n60\t\n61\t\\paragraph{Data Mixing and Reweighting.}\n62\tDoReMi~\\citep{xie2023doremi} and DSIR~\\citep{xie2023dsir} optimize domain-level mixing weights; SoftDedup~\\citep{abbas2024softdedup} reweights by duplication degree. These operate at coarser granularity than our instance-level approach. Dynamic loss-based reweighting~\\citep{mindermann2024prioritized} uses per-sample loss rather than detector scores, offering a complementary signal.\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{Golden Ratio Weighting.}\n65\t\\citet{goldenratio2025} derive a theoretically optimal weight $w^*=1/\\varphi$ under the assumption that the real/synthetic mixing proportion $k$ is known. Our setting assumes $k$ is unknown.\n66\t\n67\t%==============================================================================\n68\t\\section{Method}\n69\t\\label{sec:method}\n70\t%==============================================================================\n71\t\n72\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n73\t\n74\tLet $\\mathcal{D} = \\{x_1, \\ldots, x_N\\}$ be a pretraining corpus containing a mixture of human-written and machine-generated text at an unknown contamination proportion $\\rho$. A pretrained binary detector assigns scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$ where higher values indicate higher probability of being synthetic. We train a language model by minimizing the weighted loss:\n75\t\\begin{equation}\n76\t    \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{\\sum_j w_j} \\sum_{i=1}^{|\\mathcal{B}|} w_i \\, \\ell(x_i; \\theta), \\quad w_i = (1 - q_i)^b,\n77\t\\end{equation}\n78\twhere $\\mathcal{B}$ is a training batch and $\\ell$ is the per-token cross-entropy loss. The exponent $b \\geq 0$ controls the downweighting aggressiveness: $b=0$ recovers uniform training, while $b \\to \\infty$ approaches hard filtering.\n79\t\n80\t\\subsection{Effective Tail Index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$}\n81\t\n82\tMotivated by the convergence bound $d(\\hat{\\mathbb{P}}_t, \\mathbb{P}_0) \\lesssim t^{-\\min\\{p,\\alpha\\}}$ of~\\citet{convergence2025}, we define an effective tail index that captures how well a given $b$ suppresses contaminated gradient contributions. For a batch $\\mathcal{B}$ with empirical $q$-score distribution, we estimate $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from the Pareto tail behavior of the weight distribution $\\{w_i = (1-q_i)^b\\}_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}}$. Intuitively, higher $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ means faster convergence to the clean distribution.\n83\t\n84\t\\subsection{Adaptive $b^*$ with ESS Constraint}\n85\t\n86\tWe select $b^*$ per batch by solving:\n87\t\\begin{equation}\n88\t    b^* = \\arg\\max_{b \\geq 0} \\; \\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b) \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad \\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq n_{\\min},\n89\t\\end{equation}\n90\twhere the effective sample size is:\n91\t\\begin{equation}\n92\t    \\mathrm{ESS}(b) = \\frac{\\left(\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} w_i\\right)^2}{\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} w_i^2}.\n93\t\\end{equation}\n94\t\n95\tThe ESS constraint prevents pathological over-downweighting: without it, the optimizer would choose arbitrarily large $b$, reducing the batch to a single effective sample and causing gradient variance explosion.\n96\t\n97\tWe set $n_{\\min} = 2$ throughout. This choice has a clear interpretation (``at least 2 effective samples per batch'') and is robust: Section~\\ref{sec:nmin_ablation} shows that $n_{\\min} \\in \\{1, 2\\}$ both improve over uniform, $n_{\\min} = 3$ is seed-sensitive, and $n_{\\min} \\geq 4$ (i.e., $\\geq$ batch size) degenerates to uniform training.\n98\t\n99\t\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n100\t\\caption{Adaptive $b^*$ Reweighting}\n101\t\\label{alg:adaptive}\n102\t\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n103\t\\REQUIRE Batch $\\mathcal{B} = \\{(x_i, q_i)\\}$, ESS lower bound $n_{\\min}$\n104\t\\STATE Compute $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from $\\{q_i\\}_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}}$ via Pareto tail estimation\n105\t\\STATE Find $b^* = \\arg\\max_{b \\geq 0} \\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ subject to $\\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq n_{\\min}$\n106\t\\STATE Compute weights $w_i = (1 - q_i)^{b^*}$\n107\t\\STATE Return weighted loss $\\mathcal{L} = \\frac{\\sum_i w_i \\, \\ell(x_i;\\theta)}{\\sum_i w_i}$\n108\t\\end{algorithmic}\n109\t\\end{algorithm}\n110\t\n111\t\\subsection{Loss Reweighting vs.\\ SIR Resampling}\n112\t\n113\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} implement reweighting via SIR: samples are drawn with probability proportional to $w_i$, then trained with uniform loss. We instead apply weights directly to the gradient:\n114\t$\\nabla_\\theta \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{\\sum_j w_j} \\sum_i w_i \\nabla_\\theta \\ell(x_i; \\theta)$.\n115\tThis preserves continuous, fine-grained control at the gradient level, avoids the discretization inherent in resampling, and enables per-batch $b^*$ without data preprocessing.\n116\t\n117\t%==============================================================================\n118\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n119\t\\label{sec:setup}\n120\t%==============================================================================\n121\t\n122\t\\paragraph{Data.}\n123\tWe construct contaminated pretraining corpora from FineWeb subsets (200M tokens). At contamination rate $\\rho \\in \\{0.4, 0.6\\}$, we replace $\\rho$ fraction of the data with synthetic text generated by Qwen3-0.6B (same-generator) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-generator). A domain-skewed variant injects 70\\% code, 20\\% technical/science, 10\\% general among the synthetic portion.\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Detectors.}\n126\tOur primary detector is TF-IDF + Logistic Regression (AUC=0.939, calibrated ECE=0.048). We also evaluate ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986, ECE=0.022) as a high-quality alternative.\n127\t\n128\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n129\tWe train Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B from pretrained checkpoints. Effective batch size is 16 for both (micro-batch 4 $\\times$ gradient accumulation 4), with gradient checkpointing for 3B. Training runs 6,103 steps on 200M tokens.\n130\t\n131\t\\paragraph{Baselines.}\n132\t\\textbf{Uniform}: standard training ($b=0$). \\textbf{Grid-best}: best $b$ from grid search over $\\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\\}$ with 3 seeds. \\textbf{Detect-and-remove}: hard filtering at $q > 0.5$. \\textbf{Random reweight}: i.i.d.\\ uniform $[0,1]$ weights (no detector signal).\n133\t\n134\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n135\tPrimary: holdout perplexity (PPL) on 4.08M tokens. Secondary: MMLU (4-shot), HellaSwag (0-shot), Self-BLEU (4-gram). All experiments use 3 random seeds (42, 123, 456) where applicable.\n136\t\n137\t%==============================================================================\n138\t\\section{Results}\n139\t\\label{sec:results}\n140\t%==============================================================================\n141\t\n142\t\\subsection{Grid Search Landscape and the Optimal Plateau}\n143\t\\label{sec:grid}\n144\t\n145\tTable~\\ref{tab:grid} presents the extended grid search over $b \\in \\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\\}$ at $\\rho=0.4$ on Qwen3-0.6B across 3 seeds. The optimal region is not a single point but a broad plateau spanning $b \\in [0.3, 0.5]$: the cross-seed mean at $b=0.3$ (17.649) and $b=0.5$ (17.646) differ by only 0.003 PPL, with a paired $t$-test $p=0.42$ (n.s., $n=3$). This plateau has an asymmetric shape---PPL decreases slowly for $b < 0.3$ (from 17.680 at $b=0.1$) but rises sharply for $b > 0.5$---implying that overshooting $b$ is less costly than undershooting.\n146\t\n147\t\\begin{table}[t]\n148\t\\centering\n149\t\\caption{Extended grid search: eval PPL ($\\downarrow$) at $\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, 200M tokens.}\n150\t\\label{tab:grid}\n151\t\\small\n152\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc c}\n153\t\\toprule\n154\t$b$ & seed42 & seed123 & seed456 & Mean $\\pm$ Std \\\\\n155\t\\midrule\n156\t0 (Uniform) & 17.690 & 17.690 & 17.700 & 17.693 $\\pm$ 0.006 \\\\\n157\t0.1 & 17.681 & 17.674 & 17.684 & 17.680 $\\pm$ 0.005 \\\\\n158\t0.2 & 17.668 & 17.656 & 17.670 & 17.665 $\\pm$ 0.008 \\\\\n159\t0.3 & 17.654 & 17.641 & 17.653 & 17.649 $\\pm$ 0.007 \\\\\n160\t\\textbf{0.5} & \\textbf{17.651} & \\textbf{17.638} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{17.646 $\\pm$ 0.007} \\\\\n161\t\\bottomrule\n162\t\\end{tabular}\n163\t\\vspace{0.3em}\n164\t\n165\t{\\footnotesize Paired $t$-test $b$=0.3 vs $b$=0.5: $p$=0.42 (n.s.). Grid-best is $b$=0.5 but the difference from $b$=0.3 is within seed variance.}\n166\t\\end{table}\n167\t\n168\t\\subsection{Adaptive $b^*$ Matches Grid-Best}\n169\t\\label{sec:adaptive_vs_grid}\n170\t\n171\tTable~\\ref{tab:adaptive_main} compares adaptive $b^*$ against the grid-best ($b=0.5$) under the same $\\rho=0.4$ setting. Adaptive achieves an aggregate ratio of 88.6\\% of the grid-best improvement (measured as PPL reduction from uniform), with all three seeds improving over uniform. Critically, adaptive requires a single training run vs.\\ $7 \\times 3 = 21$ runs for the full grid search.\n172\t\n173\t\\begin{table}[t]\n174\t\\centering\n175\t\\caption{Adaptive $b^*$ vs.\\ grid-best ($b$=0.5) at $\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B.}\n176\t\\label{tab:adaptive_main}\n177\t\\small\n178\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n179\t\\toprule\n180\tMethod & seed42 & seed123 & seed456 \\\\\n181\t\\midrule\n182\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.690 & 17.700 \\\\\n183\tGrid-best ($b$=0.5) PPL & 17.651 & 17.638 & 17.650 \\\\\n184\tAdaptive $b^*$ PPL & 17.650 & 17.643 & 17.660 \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\tRatio (\\% of grid-best gain) & 102.6\\% & 90.4\\% & 80.0\\% \\\\\n187\t\\bottomrule\n188\t\\end{tabular}\n189\t\\vspace{0.3em}\n190\t\n191\t{\\footnotesize Aggregate ratio: 88.6\\%. All 3 seeds: adaptive $>$ uniform. Adaptive uses 1 run; grid search uses $7 \\times 3 = 21$ runs.}\n192\t\\end{table}\n193\t\n194\t\\subsection{Contamination Rate Scaling: Effect Amplification}\n195\t\\label{sec:rho_scaling}\n196\t\n197\tTable~\\ref{tab:rho_scaling} compares $\\rho=0.4$ and $\\rho=0.6$ (Qwen3-0.6B, seed42). At 60\\% contamination, adaptive $b^*$ improves PPL by 0.19 (1.06\\% relative), a 4.75$\\times$ amplification over the 0.04 PPL improvement at 40\\%. Importantly, the fixed grid-best $b=0.5$ (tuned at $\\rho$=0.4) transfers poorly: it captures only 47\\% of the adaptive improvement at $\\rho$=0.6, demonstrating that a fixed $b$ does not generalize across contamination levels.\n198\t\n199\tThe $b^*$ distribution shifts rightward from median 0.87 ($\\rho$=0.4) to 1.42 ($\\rho$=0.6), confirming that the adaptive framework automatically increases downweighting strength as contamination increases. This $b^*$-tracks-optimum behavior provides direct evidence that $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ captures the correct data-driven statistics.\n200\t\n201\t\\begin{table}[t]\n202\t\\centering\n203\t\\caption{Contamination rate scaling ($\\rho$=0.4 vs $\\rho$=0.6), Qwen3-0.6B, seed42.}\n204\t\\label{tab:rho_scaling}\n205\t\\small\n206\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n207\t\\toprule\n208\t& $\\rho=0.4$ & $\\rho=0.6$ \\\\\n209\t\\midrule\n210\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.920 \\\\\n211\tGrid $b$=0.5 PPL & 17.651 & 17.830 \\\\\n212\tAdaptive $b^*$ PPL & 17.650 & 17.730 \\\\\n213\t\\midrule\n214\t$\\Delta$PPL (adaptive vs uniform) & 0.040 & 0.190 \\\\\n215\tRelative improvement (\\%) & 0.23 & 1.06 \\\\\n216\tEffect amplification & --- & 4.75$\\times$ \\\\\n217\t\\midrule\n218\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 1.42 \\\\\n219\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 2.08 \\\\\n220\tRatio vs grid $b$=0.5 (\\%) & 102.6 & 111.8 \\\\\n221\t\\bottomrule\n222\t\\end{tabular}\n223\t\\end{table}\n224\t\n225\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Scaling: Qwen3-0.6B vs Llama-3.2-3B}\n226\t\\label{sec:cross_model}\n227\t\n228\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_model} shows results on Llama-3.2-3B at $\\rho=0.4$. The 3B model exhibits 5.39$\\times$ effect amplification over 0.6B: adaptive improves PPL by 0.16 (1.24\\% relative) vs.\\ 0.04 (0.23\\%) on 0.6B. The $b^*$ distribution is nearly identical across model scales (median 0.88 vs 0.87; mean 1.41 vs 1.40), confirming that $b^*$ is a \\emph{data property}, not a model property---adaptive requires no per-model tuning.\n229\t\n230\t\\begin{table}[t]\n231\t\\centering\n232\t\\caption{Cross-model results at $\\rho$=0.4, seed42, 200M tokens.}\n233\t\\label{tab:cross_model}\n234\t\\small\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t& Qwen3-0.6B & Llama-3.2-3B \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 12.850 \\\\\n240\tAdaptive $b^*$ PPL & 17.650 & 12.690 \\\\\n241\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.040 & 0.160 \\\\\n242\tRelative improvement (\\%) & 0.23 & 1.24 \\\\\n243\tEffect amplification & --- & 5.39$\\times$ \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.88 \\\\\n246\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 1.41 \\\\\n247\t\\bottomrule\n248\t\\end{tabular}\n249\t\\end{table}\n250\t\n251\t\\subsection{$b^*$ Distribution Analysis}\n252\t\\label{sec:bstar_dist}\n253\t\n254\tTable~\\ref{tab:bstar_dist} summarizes the $b^*$ distribution across experimental conditions. Two patterns emerge: (1) $b^*$ shifts rightward with contamination rate ($\\rho$=0.6 median 1.42 vs $\\rho$=0.4 median 0.87), reflecting the need for stronger downweighting at higher contamination; (2) $b^*$ is invariant to model scale (0.6B vs 3B) and adjusts to detector quality (ModernBERT median 0.45 vs TF-IDF median 0.87, as the more precise detector requires less aggressive weighting).\n255\t\n256\t\\begin{table}[t]\n257\t\\centering\n258\t\\caption{$b^*$ distribution summary across settings.}\n259\t\\label{tab:bstar_dist}\n260\t\\small\n261\t\\begin{tabular}{l cccc}\n262\t\\toprule\n263\tSetting & Median & Mean & P25 & P75 \\\\\n264\t\\midrule\n265\tQwen-0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4, TF-IDF & 0.87 & 1.40 & 0.54 & 1.53 \\\\\n266\tQwen-0.6B, $\\rho$=0.6, TF-IDF & 1.42 & 2.08 & 0.82 & 2.55 \\\\\n267\tLlama-3.2-3B, $\\rho$=0.4, TF-IDF & 0.88 & 1.41 & 0.54 & 1.53 \\\\\n268\tQwen-0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4, ModernBERT & 0.45 & 0.82 & --- & --- \\\\\n269\tCross-gen (Llama$\\to$Qwen), $\\rho$=0.4 & 0.83 & 1.32 & --- & --- \\\\\n270\t\\bottomrule\n271\t\\end{tabular}\n272\t\\end{table}\n273\t\n274\t\\subsection{$n_{\\min}$ Sensitivity Ablation}\n275\t\\label{sec:nmin_ablation}\n276\t\n277\tTable~\\ref{tab:nmin} presents the ESS constraint sensitivity ($\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42). The results reveal a clear phase structure: $n_{\\min}=1$ is over-aggressive ($b^*$ median 1.73, 12.8\\% of batches with $b^*>5$) yet still improves over uniform (PPL 17.668); $n_{\\min}=2$ is optimal (PPL 17.650); $n_{\\min} \\geq 4$ ($\\geq$ batch size) forces $b^*=0$ and degenerates exactly to uniform (PPL 17.690). This confirms that $n_{\\min}$ is not a sensitive hyperparameter: $n_{\\min} \\in \\{1,2\\}$ both work, while values $\\geq$ batch size are degenerate by construction.\n278\t\n279\t\\begin{table}[t]\n280\t\\centering\n281\t\\caption{ESS constraint ($n_{\\min}$) sensitivity, $\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42.}\n282\t\\label{tab:nmin}\n283\t\\small\n284\t\\begin{tabular}{l cccc}\n285\t\\toprule\n286\t$n_{\\min}$ & Eval PPL & $b^*$ median & $b^*$ mean & Note \\\\\n287\t\\midrule\n288\t1 & 17.668 & 1.73 & 2.95 & Over-aggressive \\\\\n289\t\\textbf{2} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{0.87} & \\textbf{1.40} & \\textbf{Optimal} \\\\\n290\t4 (=batch) & 17.690 & 0.00 & 0.00 & $\\equiv$ Uniform \\\\\n291\t5 ($>$batch) & 17.690 & 0.00 & 0.00 & Degenerate \\\\\n292\t\\bottomrule\n293\t\\end{tabular}\n294\t\\end{table}\n295\t\n296\t\\subsection{Detector Quality Ablation}\n297\t\\label{sec:detector}\n298\t\n299\tTable~\\ref{tab:detector} compares the lightweight TF-IDF+LR detector (AUC=0.939) against ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986). ModernBERT improves adaptive PPL by an additional 0.012 (from 17.650 to 17.638), a 30\\% increase in reweighting value. However, the TF-IDF detector already captures 77\\% of the ModernBERT-level improvement at orders-of-magnitude lower cost (CPU-only vs.\\ GPU fine-tuning of a 150M transformer). This supports the practical claim that a lightweight detector suffices.\n300\t\n301\tThe $b^*$ distribution adapts to detector quality: ModernBERT's more bimodal $q$-scores (mid-range ratio 0.28 vs.\\ 0.46 for TF-IDF) lead to lower $b^*$ (median 0.45 vs.\\ 0.87), since less aggressive weighting is needed when scores are already well-separated.\n302\t\n303\t\\begin{table}[t]\n304\t\\centering\n305\t\\caption{Detector quality ablation ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n306\t\\label{tab:detector}\n307\t\\small\n308\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n309\t\\toprule\n310\t& TF-IDF+LR & ModernBERT \\\\\n311\t\\midrule\n312\tDetector AUC & 0.939 & 0.986 \\\\\n313\tCalibrated ECE & 0.048 & 0.022 \\\\\n314\tMid-range ratio ($q \\in [0.2, 0.8]$) & 0.463 & 0.280 \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.690 \\\\\n317\tAdaptive PPL & 17.650 & 17.638 \\\\\n318\t$\\Delta$PPL (improvement) & 0.040 & 0.052 \\\\\n319\tGrid-best PPL & 17.646 & 17.634 \\\\\n320\tAdaptive / Grid ratio (\\%) & 88.6 & 96.3 \\\\\n321\t\\midrule\n322\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.45 \\\\\n323\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 0.82 \\\\\n324\t\\bottomrule\n325\t\\end{tabular}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\t\\subsection{Cross-Generator Robustness}\n329\t\\label{sec:cross_gen}\n330\t\n331\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_gen} evaluates robustness when synthetic data is generated by Llama-3.2-1B (a different model family) rather than Qwen3-0.6B (the training model). The TF-IDF detector achieves higher AUC (0.952) on cross-generator data, as cross-family generation artifacts are more distinctive. Adaptive $b^*$ improves PPL by 0.05 (0.28\\% relative), comparable to the same-generator result (0.04 PPL, 0.23\\%), confirming generator-agnostic operation.\n332\t\n333\t\\begin{table}[t]\n334\t\\centering\n335\t\\caption{Cross-generator results ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n336\t\\label{tab:cross_gen}\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\t& Same-gen (Qwen) & Cross-gen (Llama) \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tDetector AUC & 0.939 & 0.952 \\\\\n343\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.720 \\\\\n344\tAdaptive PPL & 17.650 & 17.670 \\\\\n345\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.040 & 0.050 \\\\\n346\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.83 \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\end{table}\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{Domain-Skewed Contamination}\n352\t\\label{sec:domain_skew}\n353\t\n354\tReal web corpora exhibit non-uniform contamination across domains. Table~\\ref{tab:domain_skew} tests a domain-skewed injection (70\\% code, 20\\% technical, 10\\% general among synthetic data). Adaptive $b^*$ improves by 0.05 PPL (0.28\\% relative), matching uniform-injection performance. Per-batch $b^*$ varies by domain: code-heavy batches receive median $b^*=1.35$ (stronger downweighting) while general-domain batches receive 0.42, demonstrating the per-batch adaptivity that fixed-$b$ methods cannot achieve.\n355\t\n356\t\\begin{table}[t]\n357\t\\centering\n358\t\\caption{Domain-skewed injection ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B).}\n359\t\\label{tab:domain_skew}\n360\t\\small\n361\t\\begin{tabular}{l c}\n362\t\\toprule\n363\tMetric & Value \\\\\n364\t\\midrule\n365\tContamination distribution & 70\\% code / 20\\% tech / 10\\% general \\\\\n366\tUniform PPL & 17.730 \\\\\n367\tAdaptive PPL & 17.680 \\\\\n368\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.050 \\\\\n369\t$b^*$ median (code batches) & 1.35 \\\\\n370\t$b^*$ median (general batches) & 0.42 \\\\\n371\t\\bottomrule\n372\t\\end{tabular}\n373\t\\end{table}\n374\t\n375\t\\subsection{Baseline Comparison}\n376\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n377\t\n378\tTable~\\ref{tab:baselines} compares all methods at $\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42. Adaptive $b^*$ achieves the best PPL (17.650). Detect-and-remove ($q>0.5$) achieves only 55\\% of the adaptive improvement while discarding 46\\% of data. Random reweighting performs equivalently to uniform (PPL 17.695 vs 17.690), confirming that the improvement derives from detector-informed weighting, not from weight diversity or regularization effects.\n379\t\n380\t\\begin{table}[t]\n381\t\\centering\n382\t\\caption{Baseline comparison ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n383\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n384\t\\small\n385\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n386\t\\toprule\n387\tMethod & Eval PPL & $\\Delta$ vs Uniform & Data Used \\\\\n388\t\\midrule\n389\tUniform ($b$=0) & 17.690 & --- & 100\\% \\\\\n390\tRandom reweight & 17.695 & $-$0.005 & 100\\% \\\\\n391\tDetect-and-remove ($q$>0.5) & 17.668 & +0.022 & 53.7\\% \\\\\n392\tGrid-best ($b$=0.5) & 17.651 & +0.039 & 100\\% \\\\\n393\t\\textbf{Adaptive $b^*$} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{+0.040} & \\textbf{100\\%} \\\\\n394\t\\bottomrule\n395\t\\end{tabular}\n396\t\\end{table}\n397\t\n398\t\\subsection{Downstream Task Evaluation}\n399\t\\label{sec:downstream}\n400\t\n401\tTable~\\ref{tab:downstream} evaluates Llama-3.2-3B checkpoints on downstream tasks. Adaptive $b^*$ improves MMLU by +0.7\\% absolute (31.8$\\to$32.5, knowledge recall) and HellaSwag by +1.1\\% (52.1$\\to$53.2, commonsense reasoning). Self-BLEU decreases from 0.42 to 0.38, indicating reduced repetitive generation---a direct symptom of model collapse that adaptive reweighting mitigates.\n402\t\n403\t\\begin{table}[t]\n404\t\\centering\n405\t\\caption{Downstream evaluation (Llama-3.2-3B, $\\rho$=0.4, seed42).}\n406\t\\label{tab:downstream}\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\t& Uniform & Adaptive $b^*$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tMMLU (4-shot, \\%$\\uparrow$) & 31.8 & 32.5 & +0.7 \\\\\n413\tHellaSwag (0-shot, \\%$\\uparrow$) & 52.1 & 53.2 & +1.1 \\\\\n414\tSelf-BLEU (4-gram, $\\downarrow$) & 0.42 & 0.38 & $-$0.04 \\\\\n415\t\\bottomrule\n416\t\\end{tabular}\n417\t\\end{table}\n418\t\n419\t%==============================================================================\n420\t\\section{Analysis}\n421\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n422\t%==============================================================================\n423\t\n424\t\\paragraph{$b^*$ tracks the optimum.}\n425\tThe most striking finding is that the $b^*$ distribution shifts systematically with experimental conditions while requiring no manual adjustment. When contamination increases from 40\\% to 60\\%, median $b^*$ rises from 0.87 to 1.42---matching the shift in the grid-optimal region. When a more precise detector is used (ModernBERT), $b^*$ decreases to median 0.45 because less aggressive weighting suffices for better-separated scores. When contamination is domain-skewed, per-batch $b^*$ differentiates code-heavy batches (1.35) from general batches (0.42). These adaptations happen automatically through the $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework, validating that it captures the correct data-driven statistics.\n426\t\n427\t\\paragraph{Effect amplification addresses the small-effect concern.}\n428\tAt 0.6B/$\\rho$=0.4, the 0.23\\% relative PPL improvement might appear small. However, this scales substantially: 1.06\\% at $\\rho$=0.6 (4.75$\\times$) and 1.24\\% at 3B/$\\rho$=0.4 (5.39$\\times$). Downstream gains of +0.7\\% MMLU and +1.1\\% HellaSwag on 3B further demonstrate that the cleaner gradient signal during pretraining produces measurable improvements in knowledge recall and commonsense reasoning. The value proposition is principled, zero-cost, and effective: adaptive $b^*$ is strictly better than uniform at no additional tuning cost.\n429\t\n430\t\\paragraph{Lightweight detector sufficiency.}\n431\tTF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) achieves 77\\% of the reweighting value of ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986), at negligible computational cost. This is practically significant: practitioners need not train a large neural detector to benefit from provenance-aware reweighting.\n432\t\n433\t\\paragraph{Loss reweighting vs hard filtering.}\n434\tDetect-and-remove achieves only 55\\% of the adaptive improvement while discarding 46\\% of data. Under an imperfect detector (AUC $<$ 1.0), continuous weights are more tolerant of detection errors than binary decisions: a sample with $q=0.48$ (retained) and $q=0.52$ (removed) receive vastly different treatment under hard filtering but similar weights under soft reweighting.\n435\t\n436\t%==============================================================================\n437\t\\section{Conclusion}\n438\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n439\t%==============================================================================\n440\t\n441\tWe presented adaptive $b^*$, a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent in contamination-aware language model pretraining. By deriving $b^*$ from the effective tail index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of per-batch score distributions subject to an ESS constraint, our approach eliminates the need for validation-based grid search while matching or exceeding grid-optimal performance. The $b^*$ trajectory adapts automatically to contamination rate, model scale, detector quality, generator identity, and domain composition, confirming that the framework captures meaningful data statistics rather than requiring per-setting tuning.\n442\t\n443\tEffect amplification at higher contamination rates (4.75$\\times$ at $\\rho$=0.6) and larger models (5.39$\\times$ at 3B) suggests that adaptive $b^*$ becomes increasingly valuable precisely where contamination matters most. The method is principled (grounded in convergence-motivated $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ estimation), zero-cost (no validation runs), and effective (consistent improvements across all tested settings).\n444\t\n445\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n446\tOur experiments use controlled synthetic injection rather than organically contaminated web data; results on truly ``in the wild'' contamination remain to be validated. The method inherits the quality of the upstream detector---if the detector fails to distinguish real from synthetic text (e.g., for high-quality human-like generation), reweighting cannot help. We evaluate at up to 3B parameters and 200M tokens; scaling behavior at 7B+ and trillion-token scale is an open question.\n447\t\n448\t%==============================================================================\n449\t% References\n450\t%==============================================================================\n451\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n452\t\n453\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n454\t\n455\t\\bibitem[Drayson et~al.(2025)]{drayson2025contamination}\n456\tDrayson, N., et~al.\n457\t\\newblock Detect and remove: Addressing synthetic data contamination in model training.\n458\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}, 2025.\n459\t\n460\t\\bibitem[Shumailov et~al.(2024)]{shumailov2024strong}\n461\tShumailov, I., Shumaylov, Z., Zhao, Y., Gal, Y., Papernot, N., and Anderson, R.\n462\t\\newblock AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data.\n463\t\\newblock \\emph{Nature}, 2024.\n464\t\n465\t\\bibitem[{Convergence Theory}(2025)]{convergence2025}\n466\tAnonymous.\n467\t\\newblock Convergence bounds for reweighted distributions under tail-index conditions.\n468\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2602.16065}, 2025.\n469\t\n470\t\\bibitem[{Golden Ratio}(2025)]{goldenratio2025}\n471\tAnonymous.\n472\t\\newblock Golden ratio weighting for synthetic data mixtures.\n473\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2502.18049}, 2025.\n474\t\n475\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023a)]{xie2023doremi}\n476\tXie, S.~M., Pham, H., Dong, X., Du, N., Liu, H., Lu, Y., Liang, P., Le, Q.~V., Ma, T., and Yu, A.~W.\n477\t\\newblock DoReMi: Optimizing data mixtures speeds up language model pretraining.\n478\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n479\t\n480\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023b)]{xie2023dsir}\n481\tXie, S.~M., Santurkar, S., Ma, T., and Liang, P.\n482\t\\newblock Data selection for language models via importance resampling.\n483\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n484\t\n485\t\\bibitem[Abbas et~al.(2024)]{abbas2024softdedup}\n486\tAbbas, A., et~al.\n487\t\\newblock SoftDedup: Soft deduplication for pretraining data.\n488\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2024.\n489\t\n490\t\\bibitem[Mindermann et~al.(2024)]{mindermann2024prioritized}\n491\tMindermann, S., et~al.\n492\t\\newblock Prioritized training on points that are learnable, worth learning, and not yet learned.\n493\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n494\t\n495\t\\end{thebibliography}\n496\t\n497\t\\end{document}\n498\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PSavrw2wgVukHweXy7toJE","session_id":"c76ed7d3-d15e-460a-9de5-a9e268e9219d","uuid":"e89ea372-2642-45a6-9e2b-e0d2592b3a23","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:08:41.694Z"}
