{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a2d49292ac534e039","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PSavrw2wgVukHweXy7toJE","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":94416,"tool_uses":15,"duration_ms":116794},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"5d923c92-e3f4-46cf-b7b6-fd7dfcaa5ecd","session_id":"c76ed7d3-d15e-460a-9de5-a9e268e9219d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UEHhxYv3Q6BKpZ824NZ83Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FeykN3UgzZ4dywK5DNcNYi","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Reviewer 2: Novelty + Related Work + Story Consistency\n\n## Paper Summary (Positioning and Novelty)\n\nThe paper proposes adaptive b*, a per-batch method for automatically selecting the reweighting exponent in the detect-and-reweight framework of Drayson et al. (EMNLP 2025), using an effective tail index alpha_eff(b) maximized subject to an ESS constraint. The core novelty is narrow but clearly delineated: the weighting formula w=(1-q)^b is borrowed from Drayson, and the contribution is a principled, zero-validation-cost mechanism for choosing b. The paper positions itself as complementary to rather than replacing Drayson, targeting a different scenario (web pretraining with unknown contamination proportion, vs. recursive training with validation-based tuning). The differentiation from Golden Ratio Weighting (known k required) provides a secondary positioning axis.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**S1. Clear and honest differentiation from Drayson et al.**\nThe paper explicitly acknowledges that it uses the same weighting formula as Drayson et al. and does not overstate its novelty. The positioning table in the method (SIR vs. loss reweighting, fixed vs. adaptive b, validation-based vs. zero-cost) is well-structured. This transparency is commendable and makes the contribution credible.\n\n**S2. b*-tracks-optimum is a compelling empirical narrative.**\nThe observation that b* shifts rightward with contamination rate (median 0.87 at rho=0.4 to 1.42 at rho=0.6), decreases with better detectors (0.45 for ModernBERT), and differentiates per domain (code 1.35 vs. general 0.42) provides multi-axis evidence that the alpha_eff(b) framework captures meaningful data-driven statistics. This is the strongest evidence for Claim 1.\n\n**S3. Well-designed ablation structure.**\nThe n_min ablation (Section 5.6) reveals a clean phase structure (over-aggressive -> optimal -> seed-sensitive -> degenerate) that is both interpretable and reassuring. The random reweighting baseline rules out the trivial explanation that any weight diversity helps. The detect-and-remove comparison isolates the advantage of continuous over binary decisions.\n\n**S4. The \"effect amplification\" narrative addresses the obvious weakness proactively.**\nRather than hiding the small effect at rho=0.4/0.6B (0.23% relative PPL), the paper explicitly discusses it and shows scaling behavior at higher contamination (4.75x) and larger models (5.39x). This is effective rhetorical strategy backed by consistent evidence across conditions.\n\n**S5. Lightweight detector finding has practical value.**\nTF-IDF+LR capturing 77% of ModernBERT reweighting value at negligible cost is a useful practical insight, even if secondary to the main contribution.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**W1. [Major, idea-level] The \"theoretical framework\" contribution is more of a heuristic design choice than a theory.**\nClaim 1 calls alpha_eff(b) estimation with ESS constraints a \"theoretical framework\" and a \"principled method.\" However, the paper provides no theorem, no formal connection between alpha_eff(b) and downstream convergence, and no proof that maximizing alpha_eff(b) subject to ESS >= n_min yields an optimal or near-optimal b. The convergence bound from the anonymous preprint (arXiv:2602.16065) is used only as \"motivation\" (the paper's own words), and the gap between this motivation and the actual algorithm design is not formally bridged. In its current form, alpha_eff(b) is an empirically-motivated scoring function, not a theoretical contribution. The b* selection procedure is better characterized as a well-motivated heuristic than a principled theoretical framework. Calling it \"principled\" without formal guarantees is overclaiming.\n\n**W2. [Major, idea-level] The contribution scope is narrow: an adaptive hyperparameter selection method for a single formula from prior work.**\nThe core contribution reduces to: given the pre-existing formula w=(1-q)^b, automatically select b per batch. This is a hyperparameter-free variant of an existing method. While useful, this level of contribution may be insufficient for a top venue, especially when the underlying reweighting formula, the detect-and-reweight paradigm, and even the problem framing are all from Drayson et al. The paper essentially replaces grid search over one scalar hyperparameter with an automatic estimator. The \"loss reweighting vs. SIR\" difference is presented as a secondary contribution, but this distinction is well-known in the importance sampling literature and does not constitute a novel methodological insight.\n\n**W3. [Major, presentation-level] The \"Convergence Theory\" reference (arXiv:2602.16065) is anonymous and unverifiable.**\nThis reference appears to be either fabricated or to an anonymous preprint that cannot be located. The arxiv ID prefix \"2602\" does not correspond to any valid month (February 2026?). This is the sole theoretical grounding for the alpha_eff(b) definition. If this reference is fictitious, the theoretical motivation collapses to an unjustified design choice. Even if this is a pipeline artifact in the dream simulation context, in a real submission this would be a fatal credibility issue.\n\n**W4. [Minor, idea-level] n_min = 2 is the only value that works, making it effectively a tuned hyperparameter.**\nThe ablation shows: n_min=1 is over-aggressive, n_min=2 is optimal, n_min=3 is seed-sensitive (indeed, the entire D011-D012 debugging episode was caused by n_min=3 failing), and n_min >= 4 degenerates to uniform. A \"robustness window\" of {1, 2} (two values, one suboptimal) is not particularly reassuring. The paper claims adaptive b* \"eliminates hyperparameter tuning,\" but n_min must still be chosen correctly. The batch_size=4 (micro-batch) constraint further limits the ESS dynamic range, meaning the method may behave differently at standard batch sizes (32, 64, 128).\n\n**W5. [Minor, presentation-level] Drayson et al. reference is inaccurate in the bibliography.**\nThe bibliography lists the first author as \"Drayson, N.\" with the title \"Detect and remove: Addressing synthetic data contamination in model training.\" However, the literature map and paper framing documents refer to \"George Drayson\" with the title \"Machine-Generated Text Detection Prevents Language Model Collapse.\" These inconsistencies suggest careless bibliography construction. In the paper body, the authors are cited as a different set of initials and names at different points. At a minimum, the authors should verify the exact title, author list, and venue of this critical reference.\n\n**W6. [Minor, presentation-level] The bibliography is very thin (8 references) for the breadth of claims.**\nThe paper touches on model collapse, data mixing, importance sampling theory, ESS estimation, Pareto tail estimation, loss reweighting, and synthetic text detection, but cites only 8 works. Missing references include: Pareto Smoothed Importance Sampling (Vehtari et al. 2024, the source of the ESS diagnostic the paper relies on), the Self-Consuming Generative Models / MAD literature (Alemohammad et al. 2024), Strong Model Collapse (Dohmatob et al. 2025, ICLR Spotlight -- the paper cites \"Shumailov et al. 2024\" from Nature but not the ICLR 2025 result on strong collapse that is actually more relevant to the \"even small amounts\" claim in the introduction), the Dynamic Loss-Based Sample Reweighting paper (Sow et al. ICLR 2025, the most direct comparison for loss-level reweighting), DetectGPT and Min-K% Prob (relevant detector baselines), the Beyond Model Collapse / Verification paper (Feng et al. 2024), and the Data Mixing Laws work (Ye et al. ICLR 2025). The literature map in the project files shows awareness of many of these works, but they are absent from the paper. This thinness weakens the related work section and makes the positioning appear less thorough than it actually is.\n\n**W7. [Minor, idea-level] The cross-model \"b* is a data property\" claim is not adequately tested.**\nThe paper claims b* distributions are near-identical across Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B (median 0.87 vs. 0.88). But this is by construction: if the same data with the same detector scores is used, and b* is computed purely from q-scores (independent of the model), then of course the distributions match. This does not demonstrate anything about the method's generalization; it is a tautological consequence of the algorithm design. The interesting claim would be whether the *same* b* is *optimal* for both models, which requires running grid search on 3B as well -- but no 3B grid search is presented.\n\n**W8. [Minor, presentation-level] The \"aggregate ratio\" metric is non-standard and somewhat misleading.**\nThe primary performance metric is \"ratio of grid-best gain captured by adaptive\" -- a bespoke metric. At rho=0.4/0.6B, this is 88.6%, which the paper frames positively. But this means adaptive recovers less than 90% of the improvement achievable by grid search. The absolute numbers are tiny: the difference between adaptive and grid-best is 0.004 PPL (seed42), 0.005 PPL (seed123), and 0.010 PPL (seed456). These differences are well within the noise floor established by the seed variance (std ~0.007). This makes it difficult to distinguish \"adaptive matches grid-best\" from \"everything in this range is noise.\"\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** Can you provide any formal result (even a simplified or asymptotic one) connecting the maximization of alpha_eff(b) to improved convergence or reduced distributional distance? Without this, \"principled\" seems unjustified.\n\n**Q2.** You claim n_min is not a sensitive hyperparameter, but n_min=3 caused a complete failure for seed42 (PPL = uniform, as documented in your development process). If n_min=3 is failure-prone and n_min >= 4 is degenerate, how do you recommend practitioners choose n_min for batch sizes other than 4? Is there a principled rule relating n_min to batch size?\n\n**Q3.** The effect sizes at rho=0.4 are 0.040 PPL (adaptive) vs. 0.044 PPL (grid-best), a difference of 0.004 PPL. Given seed std of ~0.007, can you provide a power analysis or statistical test demonstrating that adaptive and grid-best are statistically indistinguishable?\n\n**Q4.** For the 3B model, grid search results are not presented. How can you claim adaptive \"surpasses grid-best\" at 3B without having established what grid-best is? The imagined ratio of 114% is based on an assumed grid-best, not a measured one.\n\n**Q5.** The convergence theory reference (arXiv:2602.16065) -- can you confirm this preprint exists and is publicly accessible? The arxiv ID format is unusual.\n\n**Q6.** Drayson et al. explicitly state they do not assume known contamination proportion. Your Claim 3 positions against both Golden Ratio (requires known k) and Drayson (requires validation set). But if Drayson's b selection via validation can operate without knowing the contamination proportion, what exactly is the practical gap your Claim 3 addresses? Is it solely the absence of a validation set?\n\n**Q7.** The paper mentions the method works at \"two detector qualities\" but only tests two: a very weak one (TF-IDF, AUC=0.939) and a moderately strong one (ModernBERT, AUC=0.986). What happens when the detector is poor (AUC ~0.7-0.8)? The method's dependence on detector quality is acknowledged as a limitation but not tested in this regime.\n\n---\n\n## Preliminary Score\n\n**3 -- Borderline Reject**\n\nThe paper addresses a legitimate practical problem (automated b selection in detect-and-reweight) and presents a clean experimental setup with sensible ablations. The b*-tracks-optimum empirical finding is interesting. However, the contribution is narrow (adaptive selection of one hyperparameter in someone else's framework), the claimed \"theoretical framework\" lacks formal grounding, the core novelty -- while real -- may not reach the threshold for a top venue at ~25% acceptance rate. The thin bibliography, questionable convergence theory reference, and small absolute effect sizes compound these concerns. The paper would benefit from either (a) a formal theoretical result connecting alpha_eff to convergence, or (b) substantially broader experimental validation at realistic scale (7B+, trillion-token), or (c) a positioning shift that acknowledges the contribution as a useful engineering improvement rather than a theoretical advance.\n\n---\n\n## Confidence\n\n**4 -- High.** I have read the paper carefully and am familiar with the relevant literature on importance weighting, data mixing for LLM pretraining, and model collapse. The one area of uncertainty is whether the convergence theory reference is real, which I was unable to verify.\n","project":"provenance_weight_training","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260515T011902","overall_score":"borderline reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":49007,"cache_read_input_tokens":45216,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":49007,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PSavrw2wgVukHweXy7toJE","session_id":"c76ed7d3-d15e-460a-9de5-a9e268e9219d","uuid":"26fc5af2-00ee-4b4a-b2af-f0f5fe088615"}
